var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"processes/albedo/#Albedo","page":"Albedo","text":"Surface albedo is stored in model.state.albedo[idx] and is updated before the energy solve. The energy solver then uses it for the absorbed shortwave radiation Q_sw = (1-alpha)Q_mathrmswdown.\n\nThe scheme is chosen with the albedo_scheme slot of Params, e.g. Params(albedo_scheme = AgingAlbedo()) (TOML name in parentheses):\n\nalbedo_scheme Behaviour\nDynamicAlbedo() (default, dynamic) prognostic BESSI albedo with temperature aging, wetness and gradual snowfall brightening\nConstantAlbedo() (constant) alpha_dry, alpha_wet or alpha_ice from the surface state\nAgingAlbedo() (aging) exponential decay with time since snowfall (Hoang et al., 2025)\nSemixAlbedo() (semix) two-band SEMIX snow albedo from grain size, dust, solar zenith angle and clouds\nPrescribedAlbedo() (prescribed) prescribed_albedo forcing for every column and step\n\nThe albedos shared by the schemes are fields of Params:\n\nParameter Default Meaning\nalpha_dry 0.81 dry (fresh) snow albedo\nalpha_wet 0.70 wet (aged) snow albedo\nalpha_ice 0.40 bare-ice albedo\nmax_lwc_albedo 0.1 surface liquid-water content at which the albedo reaches alpha_wet\n\nThe coefficients of a scheme are fields of its type, e.g. the aging time scales of AgingAlbedo (see Parameters). The defaults are calibrated against MAR over Greenland. The Antarctic setup ParamsAISv1() uses alpha_dry=0.87, alpha_wet=0.82.\n\nIn the formulas below, T_1 is the temperature of the first snow layer (temperature[1]), not the interface temperature Tsrf.","title":"Albedo"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/albedo/#Constant-Scheme","page":"Albedo","text":"With ConstantAlbedo(), the code uses:\n\nalpha_ice when no snow is present\nalpha_wet when snow is present and the surface layer is at or above T0\nalpha_dry otherwise","title":"Constant Scheme"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/albedo/#Dynamic-Scheme","page":"Albedo","text":"The dynamic scheme is the default of Params. It keeps the surface albedo between alpha_wet and alpha_dry; bare ice uses alpha_ice. The defaults alpha_dry = 0.81, alpha_wet = 0.70, and alpha_ice = 0.40 were calibrated against MAR over Greenland in coupled runs.\n\nThe dynamic scheme follows the BESSI implementation of Zolles and Born (2021), with temperature-dependent aging rates based on broadband albedo observations (Aoki et al., 2003). The previous albedo is first clamped to [alpha_wet, alpha_dry]. A column that was bare ice (alpha = 040) therefore starts from at least alpha_wet once any snow covers it.","title":"Dynamic Scheme"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/albedo/#Temperature-Aging","page":"Albedo","text":"For an existing snow surface, the code applies\n\nalpha^star =\nminleft(\nalpha^n\nalpha^n - Delta t_mathrmdays\nleft(135times 10^-3(T_1 - T_0) + 00278right)\nright)\n\nfollowed by a clamp to alpha_wet, so warm snow darkens faster than cold snow. Scaling by the elapsed time Delta t_mathrmdays keeps the aging rate consistent when a forcing step is divided into diurnal substeps.","title":"Temperature Aging"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/albedo/#Wetness-Adjustment","page":"Albedo","text":"The surface liquid-water content is diagnosed from the current pore volume:\n\nmathrmLWC_mathrmsurf =\nfracm_w  rho_wmrho - mrho_i\n\nWhen LWC_surf > 0 and max_lwc_albedo > 0, define r = clamp(LWC_surf / max_lwc_albedo, 0, 1). The wetness adjustment is\n\nalpha^n+1 = alpha_mathrmwet +\n(alpha^star - alpha_mathrmwet)(1-r)^Delta t_mathrmdays\n\nThis preserves the original one-day adjustment and makes wetness relaxation independent of subdivision at fixed wetness, in isolation from temperature aging. Zero elapsed time applies no wetness adjustment; r = 1 reaches alpha_wet for any positive elapsed time. Combined aging, snowfall, and evolving column conditions can still introduce timestep dependence.","title":"Wetness Adjustment"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/albedo/#Snowfall-Refresh","page":"Albedo","text":"Fresh snowfall brightens the surface according to\n\nalpha^n+ =\nminleft(\nalpha_mathrmdry\nalpha^n + (alpha_mathrmdry - alpha_mathrmwet)\nleft(1 - e^-Delta m_mathrmsnow3right)\nright)\n\nwhere Δm_snow is the newly added snowfall mass in kg m^-2. Small events brighten the surface gradually, whereas events of more than about 10 kg m⁻² restore an albedo close to that of fresh snow. The refresh happens during accumulation. The constant and aging schemes instead reset the albedo to alpha_dry after any snowfall.","title":"Snowfall Refresh"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/albedo/#Aging-Scheme","page":"Albedo","text":"Set albedo_scheme = AgingAlbedo() to use a snowfall-age albedo following Hoang et al. (2025). Snowfall resets model.state.snow_age_days to zero and the albedo to alpha_dry. During snow-free forcing intervals the age advances by dt_days, and the albedo relaxes towards the aged-snow value step by step,\n\nalpha^n+1 = alpha_mathrmwet +\n(alpha^n - alpha_mathrmwet)\nexpleft(-Delta t_mathrmdaystauright)\n\nwhich for constant tau is the published alpha = alpha_mathrmfirn + (alpha_mathrmfresh - alpha_mathrmfirn)exp(-ttau) with t the time since snowfall.\n\nThe scheme reuses the existing albedo constants: alpha_dry is the fresh-snow albedo, alpha_wet is the aged-snow/firn asymptote, and alpha_ice is used without surface snow. The aging timescale defaults are AgingAlbedo(cold_timescale_days=20, melting_timescale_days=2). The cold timescale is used below T0; the melting timescale is used at or above T0. A column without surface snow uses alpha_ice and has zero snow age. For time-varying surface temperature, the exponential decay is applied incrementally over each forcing interval. This preserves the published law when the timescale is constant and prevents snow from becoming brighter when the surface returns below T0. Unlike the constant scheme, reaching the melting point does not cause an instantaneous albedo jump.\n\nThe albedos are fields of Params and the two aging timescales coefficients of the scheme. For example:\n\nparams = Params(\n    albedo_scheme=AgingAlbedo(cold_timescale_days=20.0, melting_timescale_days=2.0),\n    alpha_dry=0.82,\n    alpha_wet=0.60,\n    alpha_ice=0.40,\n)\nmodel = BESSIModel(grid; forcing, params)","title":"Aging Scheme"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/albedo/#SEMIX-Scheme","page":"Albedo","text":"albedo_scheme = SemixAlbedo() uses the spectral snow albedo of SEMIX (Willeit et al., 2024). It computes visible and near-infrared albedos for direct and diffuse light from\n\nthe snow grain size, which grows with temperature and shrinks with snowfall (snow_grain_fresh, snow_grain_old, snow_0, snow_1, dT_age, f_age_t)\nthe dust concentration in the snow, from optional dust_deposition forcing and amplified during melt of the seasonal snowpack (dust_con_scale, w_snow_dust, state w_snow_max)\nthe cosine of the solar zenith angle (coszm forcing, or the daily mean from latitude and season)\nthe subgrid orographic roughness (z_sur_std forcing; Dang scheme only)\n\nfollowing Dang et al. (2015) (snow_bands = DangSnowAlbedo(), default) or Warren and Wiscombe (1980) (snow_bands = WarrenWiscombeSnowAlbedo()). The bands are combined into a broadband albedo with the cloud fraction (cloud forcing) and the visible fraction frac_vu. The coefficients in parentheses are fields of SemixAlbedo. Over bare ice the scheme uses alpha_ice, or the optional prescribed_ice_albedo forcing.","title":"SEMIX Scheme"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/albedo/#Prescribed-Scheme","page":"Albedo","text":"albedo_scheme = PrescribedAlbedo() takes the surface albedo from the prescribed_albedo forcing, which must be present for every column and step. It also applies over bare ice.","title":"Prescribed Scheme"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/albedo/#API","page":"Albedo","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/albedo/#Chion.update_surface_albedo!","page":"Albedo","text":"update_surface_albedo!(model, idx, dt_days=1)\n\nUpdate the surface albedo of column idx of a CPU model with its albedo scheme and return the new albedo. dt_days is the elapsed time of the snow aging and wetness relaxation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.update_surface_albedo!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/albedo/#Chion._constant_surface_albedo-processes-albedo","page":"Albedo","text":"_constant_surface_albedo(N_storage, mass, temperature, idx, c)\n\nReturn the constant-scheme surface albedo for column idx, switching between ice, dry snow, and wet snow according to surface state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._constant_surface_albedo"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/albedo/#Chion._surface_liquid_water_content-processes-albedo","page":"Albedo","text":"_surface_liquid_water_content(N_storage, mass, mass_w, density, idx, c)\n\nEstimate volumetric liquid-water content in the surface layer of column idx. Returns zero when the layer is empty, ice-dense, or has no pore volume.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._surface_liquid_water_content"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/albedo/#Chion._refresh_dynamic_albedo_from_snowfall!-processes-albedo","page":"Albedo","text":"_refresh_dynamic_albedo_from_snowfall!(albedo_dynamic, idx, c, snowfall_mass)\n\nRefresh the dynamic surface albedo after snowfall on column idx. Mutates albedo_dynamic[idx] and returns the updated albedo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._refresh_dynamic_albedo_from_snowfall!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/albedo/#Chion._update_aging_surface_albedo_arrays!-processes-albedo","page":"Albedo","text":"_update_aging_surface_albedo_arrays!(N_storage, mass, temperature,\n    albedo_dynamic, snow_age_days, idx, c, snowfall_rate, dt_days)\n\nUpdate snow albedo from the time elapsed since the latest snowfall event. Snowfall resets the age to zero and the albedo to alpha_dry. Otherwise snow age advances by dt_days and albedo decays exponentially toward alpha_wet, with separate cold- and melting-surface timescales. Bare columns use alpha_ice and carry zero snow age.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._update_aging_surface_albedo_arrays!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/albedo/#Chion._update_surface_albedo_arrays!-processes-albedo","page":"Albedo","text":"_update_surface_albedo_arrays!(N_storage, mass, mass_w, density, temperature, albedo_dynamic, idx, c, dt_days)\n\nUpdate the diagnosed surface albedo for column idx in-place using either the constant or dynamic albedo scheme. Dynamic snow aging and wetness relaxation are scaled by dt_days.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._update_surface_albedo_arrays!"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/refreezing/#Refreezing","page":"Refreezing","text":"go_refreezing! converts retained liquid water into solid mass using the cold content of subfreezing layers.\n\nFor the layered BESSI model, Params(refreezing_correction=1.0) multiplies the cold-content-limited refreezing capacity everywhere. The default preserves the original scheme. Values above one are an empirical correction representing an unresolved heat sink; refreezing remains capped by the liquid water available in each layer. With values above one the layer refreezes more water than its cold content allows, so the energy budget is no longer closed.","title":"Refreezing"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/refreezing/#Model-Formulation","page":"Refreezing","text":"For each active layer with solid mass, liquid water, and T < T0, the code computes\n\nQ_mathrmcold = f_mathrmrefreeze(T_0 - T)c_i m_s\nqquad\nQ_mathrmlat = m_w L_m\n\nHere f_mathrmrefreeze is refreezing_correction and defaults to one.","title":"Model Formulation"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/refreezing/#Partial-Refreezing","page":"Refreezing","text":"If Q_mathrmcold  Q_mathrmlat, only part of the water freezes:\n\nDelta m_mathrmrefreeze = fracQ_mathrmcoldL_m\n\nThe code then sets:\n\nT -> T0\nm_s -> m_s + Δm_refreeze\nm_w -> m_w - Δm_refreeze\ndensity -> min(density * (m_s + Δm_refreeze) / m_s, rho_i)","title":"Partial Refreezing"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/refreezing/#Complete-Refreezing","page":"Refreezing","text":"If Q_mathrmcold ge Q_mathrmlat, all liquid water freezes. Using the pre-update values of m_s, m_w, and T, the code sets\n\nT^new = minleft(T_0\nfracm_w L_m  c_i + m_w T_0 + T m_sm_w + m_sright)\n\nIt then updates:\n\nm_s -> m_s + m_w\nm_w -> 0\ndensity -> min(density * (m_s + m_w) / m_s, rho_i) using the pre-update liquid mass","title":"Complete Refreezing"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/refreezing/#API","page":"Refreezing","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/refreezing/#Chion.go_refreezing!","page":"Refreezing","text":"go_refreezing!(liquid_water_mass, solid_mass, snow_density, layer_temperature, melting_temperature, ice_heat_capacity, latent_heat_of_melting, ice_density; refreezing_correction=1)\n\nRun the refreezing scheme on vector-backed column data. Mutates the supplied arrays in-place and returns the refrozen mass together with released latent heat.\n\n\n\n\n\ngo_refreezing!(model, idx)\n\nRun the refreezing scheme for column idx of a CPU model. Mutates its state in place and returns the refrozen mass and released latent heat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.go_refreezing!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/refreezing/#Chion._go_refreezing!-processes-refreezing","page":"Refreezing","text":"_go_refreezing!(N_storage, mass_w, mass, density, temperature, idx, melting_temperature, ice_heat_capacity, latent_heat_of_melting, ice_density, refreezing_correction=1)\n\nRefreeze liquid water in subfreezing layers of column idx until either cold content or liquid water is exhausted. Mutates liquid water, solid mass, density, and temperature in-place and returns the refrozen mass. refreezing_correction scales the cold-content-limited refreezing capacity; values above one represent an empirical unresolved heat sink.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._go_refreezing!"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Verification-And-Validation","page":"Verification And Validation","text":"This page summarizes how the numerical core of Chion is verified against analytical solutions and conservation checks. The figures and numbers are from the Chion model description paper. Evaluation against observations and regional climate models (ESM-SnowMIP, Northern Hemisphere snow cover, Greenland and Antarctic firn and SMB) is reported there and is not repeated here.\n\nThe executable checks live in test/; run them with julia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'. Relevant files are test_longwave_consistency.jl (insulated equilibrium, graybody longwave), test_surface_energy_balance.jl (closure of the SEB diagnostics), test_case_api.jl (API and scheme selection), test_simulation.jl (the Simulation/Output workflow), test_forcing_host.jl (host forcing helpers) and test_gpu.jl (CPU/GPU agreement, needs using CUDA).","title":"Verification And Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Heat-Conduction","page":"Verification And Validation","text":"The implicit solver is tested in a single column against analytical solutions for a semi-infinite solid (Carslaw and Jaeger, 1986).\n\nStep response. A 6 m snow column (ρ = 350 kg m⁻³, 300 layers of 2 cm, Δt = 1 h) at −10 °C receives a 1 K surface step, either with a fixed surface temperature or through a finite surface exchange h(T_a - T_s) with h = 5 W m⁻² K⁻¹. The analytical solutions are\n\ntheta(zt) = operatornameerfc(eta)\nqquad\ntheta(zt) = operatornameerfc(eta) - expleft(frachzK + frach^2 kappa tK^2right)\noperatornameerfcleft(eta + frachsqrtkappa tKright)\nqquad eta = fracz2sqrtkappa t\n\nwith kappa = K(rho c_i). The maximum error is 0.63 % of the step after 1 day and below 0.11 % after 10 and 50 days (fixed surface), and 0.57 % and below 0.1 % with surface exchange.\n\n(Image: Heat-conduction step response)\n\nResponse to a 1 K surface step in homogeneous snow: (a) fixed surface temperature, (b) surface exchange with h = 5 W m⁻² K⁻¹. Analytical solutions (lines) and Chion (circles) after 1, 10 and 50 days.\n\nPeriodic forcing. A sinusoidal surface temperature (mean −15 °C, amplitude 1 K) drives (i) an annual cycle in snow, (ii) an annual cycle in 1 m of snow over the ice substrate and (iii) a daily cycle in bare ice. For a homogeneous medium the solution is a damped, delayed wave T = T_m + A e^-zdsin(omega t - zd) with d = sqrt2kappaomega. For snow over ice, a two-layer solution accounts for the reflection at the interface. In snow, amplitude and phase agree within 2.3 % and 0.015 rad. In the two-layer case errors stay below 3.4 % and 0.11 rad. In bare ice under daily forcing, the thin upper cells match. The deepest substrate cells (≳ 0.15 m, as thick as the 0.18 m daily damping depth) cannot resolve the daily wave. This is by design: the substrate stores seasonal cold content, and its coarse cells are accurate for the annual cycle.\n\n(Image: Heat-conduction periodic forcing)\n\nRelative amplitude (a–c) and phase lag (d–f) for an annual cycle in snow (a, d), an annual cycle in 1 m of snow over ice (b, e) and a daily cycle in bare ice (c, f). Lines: analytical; open circles: analytical cell means; filled circles: Chion. Dotted lines mark the snow–ice interface.\n\nConvergence. The fitted orders are 1.94 in space and 0.99 in time, close to the expected 2 and 1 of the finite-volume implicit-Euler scheme.\n\n(Image: Convergence of the heat-conduction solver)\n\n(a) RMS error against the analytical solution versus layer thickness. (b) RMS difference to a 60 s reference run versus time step.","title":"Heat Conduction"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Mass-And-Energy-Conservation","page":"Verification And Validation","text":"Mass and energy budgets are checked for every column and step of a Greenland run (365 days × 18 620 columns = 6.8 × 10⁶ samples). The change in stored solid and liquid mass plus the transfer to the ice equals precipitation minus runoff plus vapour exchange to within 2.3 × 10⁻¹¹ kg m⁻². With prescribed MAR surface fluxes, the surface energy input equals the change in heat content of the snow, firn and ice substrate plus the melt energy to within 2.5 × 10⁻¹⁰ W m⁻².\n\n(Image: Mass and energy conservation)\n\nDistribution of the absolute residual of (a) the column mass balance and (b) the energy-solve closure over all columns and daily steps of one year over Greenland.","title":"Mass And Energy Conservation"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Phase-Change","page":"Verification And Validation","text":"Melt onset. A 1 m cold snow column (−2 °C) under a constant net flux of 5 W m⁻² warms as T_s - T_mathrminit = (2FK)sqrtkappa tpi. The simulated melt onset differs by 0.049 % from the analytical one, and the melt rate afterwards stays below the limit FL_m.\nRefreezing. Liquid water added to a single layer (100 kg m⁻², 350 kg m⁻³, −10 °C) refreezes as minleft(W c_i m (T_0 - T)L_mright), at most 6.32 kg m⁻².\nIce substrate. A flux of −20 W m⁻² for 30 days followed by +20 W m⁻² for 60 days: without the substrate, bare ice melts as soon as the flux turns positive. With it, the cold content must first be removed, and the cumulative melt agrees with the net applied energy within 0.95 %.\n\n(Image: Phase-change tests)\n\n(a) Surface temperature under a constant flux and the analytical solution until melt onset. (b) Melt rate after onset and its limit F/L_m. (c) Refrozen mass against added liquid water. (d) Cumulative bare-ice melt with and without the ice substrate after a flux reversal.","title":"Phase Change"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Further-Notes","page":"Verification And Validation","text":"Fresh-Snow Density Note\nEnergy Flux Analytical Tests","title":"Further Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"model_state/#Model-State-And-Step-Flow","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"This page summarizes the state layout and the main execution order implemented in src/step.jl.\n\n(Image: Schematic of the multilayer snowpack model)\n\nSchematic of the multilayer snowpack model: forcing, the layered snow and firn column with refined near-surface layers over the thermal ice substrate, and the order of processes within one time step.","title":"Model State And Step Flow"},{"category":"section","location":"model_state/#State-Model","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"A model owns its state: model.state is the evolving prognostic state, a BESSIState, PDDState or ITMState on the model's backend. initial_state(grid, params) builds the initial state on the CPU, and a model built with state = … starts from a given one (e.g. a restart). The diagnostics and scratch arrays live in model.cache, and the fields of both are forwarded (model.mass, model.thickness). cpu_state(model) returns a host copy and set_state!(model, state) copies a host state into the model.\n\nSimulation\n├── model :: BESSIModel\n│   ├── grid :: Grid                    # ncol, optional SpatialLayout (x, y, js, is, mask),\n│   │   └── layering :: Layering        #   Ntot, mass_max/split/min, near-surface thicknesses,\n│   │                                   #   ice substrate\n│   ├── state :: BESSIState             # layers (layer, column): mass, mass_w, density,\n│   │                                   #   temperature, ice_temperature; per column N, mass_base,\n│   │                                   #   smb_ice, runoff, …, Tsrf, albedo, *_sum SEB accumulators\n│   ├── cache :: BESSICache             # workspace (heat solve, boundary_enthalpy), thickness,\n│   │                                   #   wet_mass, bulk_density, liquid_water, active columns\n│   ├── params :: Params                # constants and schemes (see Parameters)\n│   └── forcing :: AtmosphericForcing   # Forcing source, loaded year (host data, kernel fields)\n├── clock :: Clock                      # year index, step, iteration, time\n├── years, spinup, output               # what run! does (OutputConfig: run directory, outputs)\n└── io                                  # output recorders, spin-up done, wall time\n\nFor BESSIState, each column stores one active-layer count N[idx] together with layer-wise arrays for:\n\nField Meaning Units\nmass solid snow / firn / ice mass per layer kg m^-2\nmass_w retained liquid water mass per layer kg m^-2\ndensity bulk snow density per layer kg m^-3\ntemperature layer temperature K\nmass_base exported basal ice mass kg m^-2\nsmb_ice cumulative net mass forcing to the ice sheet kg m^-2\nrunoff cumulative liquid-water export kg m^-2\nmelt cumulative melted solid mass kg m^-2\nrefreezing cumulative refrozen liquid mass kg m^-2\nvapor_mass cumulative net vapour exchange (deposition and condensation positive) kg m^-2\nsublimation cumulative sublimated mass kg m^-2\nlatent_heat_flux_sum time-integrated latent heat-flux diagnostic W m^-2 day\nshortwave_down_sum, shortwave_net_sum time-integrated shortwave down and absorbed W m^-2 day\nlongwave_down_sum, longwave_net_sum time-integrated longwave down and net W m^-2 day\nsensible_heat_flux_sum, rain_heat_flux_sum time-integrated sensible heat and rain heat W m^-2 day\nTsrf snow–air (or ice–air) interface temperature K\nalbedo surface albedo used by the energy solver 1\nsnow_age_days time since the last snowfall (AgingAlbedo) day\nw_snow_max seasonal maximum snow water equivalent (SemixAlbedo) kg m^-2\nice_temperature temperatures of the ice-substrate layers, (ice_substrate_layers, column) K\n\nThe cache holds the column diagnostics thickness (m), wet_mass (kg m⁻²), bulk_density (kg m⁻³) and liquid_water (kg m⁻²), refreshed by update_diagnostics! (which run! calls at the end of every run), and the scratch arrays of the heat solve with the cumulative boundary_enthalpy (J m⁻²) of the enthalpy budget. column_profile(model, idx) returns the active layers of one column, and column_enthalpy(model, idx) its enthalpy.\n\nThe surface energy balance sums are positive into the surface. Under snow and on the ice substrate the terms are evaluated at the solved surface temperature, on bare ice without a substrate at T0. Net shortwave, net longwave, sensible, latent and rain heat add up to the energy into the column (melt and conduction), up to the linearization of the solve and the heat of snowfall, which is not among them.\n\nPDDState stores snowpack_swe, smb_ice, runoff, and pdd_sum as column vectors. Its snow reservoir is capped by H_snow_max of PDDParams. Refrozen water leaves that reservoir as superimposed ice, and smb_ice therefore contains only snow-to-ice conversion minus ice melt. For every PDD step, precipitation is partitioned according to snowfall + rainfall = Δsnowpack_swe + Δsmb_ice + Δrunoff.\n\nSnowpackForcing stores one forcing matrix per field in model-native units (K, kg m^-2 s^-1, W m^-2). Its constructor also accepts user-facing temperature and precipitation fields in Celsius and mmWE day^-1.","title":"State Model"},{"category":"section","location":"model_state/#Host/device-boundary","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"Before a KernelAbstractions launch, Chion extracts concrete named tuples containing only the arrays required by that kernel, and passes the isbits Params and Layering for inline dispatch on the schemes. Complete state, forcing, model and simulation objects are not kernel arguments.\n\nEvery launch uses an ndrange equal to the active-index or output length. Kernel entry points index that range directly under @inbounds; empty work sets are handled and validated on the host before launch.","title":"Host/device boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"model_state/#Step-Ordering","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"With the default diurnal substeps (DiurnalSubsteps()), a daily forcing step is first split into substeps (see Diurnal Shortwave Cycle); with the default longwave_scheme = CloudProxyLongwave(), the incident longwave is derived once from the daily forcing before that split. Each substep then executes, for a snow-covered column:\n\naccumulation and rainfall input; snow falling on a previously bare surface takes min(T_mathrmair T_0); layers emptied below a trace threshold are removed and their water runs off\nnear-surface remeshing to the near_surface_layer_max_thicknesses_m profile\nalbedo update\ndensification\nenergy solve of the snow column and the ice substrate\nturbulent vapour exchange (sublimation/deposition, or evaporation/ condensation at the melting point) on the surface layer\nmelt, when the energy solve diagnoses melt energy; melt in excess of the snow melts the underlying ice and runs off\npercolation\nHTESSEL liquid-water compaction, with HTESSELDensification() and liquid water remains\nrefreezing\nnear-surface remeshing again, so that the next energy solve sees the  prescribed thin top layers, and the final albedo/snow-age update\n\nIf a column has no snow after accumulation, the column step takes the bare-ice branch and skips the snow-column process chain. With an ice substrate (the default), the energy solve runs on the substrate with the top substrate layer as the surface; bare ice cools and must re-warm before melting, and melt and rain run off. Without a substrate (Layering(ice_substrate_layers = 0)), bare ice is held at the melting point and positive surface energy is converted directly into SMB loss.\n\nFor BESSI, smb_ice is the cumulative mass transferred to the ice-sheet reservoir through basal export and bare-ice surface mass changes. Monthly NetCDF smb_ice is the change in that cumulative field during the month.","title":"Step Ordering"},{"category":"section","location":"model_state/#Important-Defaults","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"The layering defaults (Layering):\n\nNtot = 15\nmass_max = 500 kg m^-2\nmass_split = 300 kg m^-2\nmass_min = 100 kg m^-2\nnear_surface_layer_max_thicknesses_m = (0.02, 0.05, 0.10, 0.30)\nice_substrate_layers = 5, ice_substrate_top_thickness_m = 0.05\n\nand of the parameters (Params):\n\nConstantFreshSnowDensity(density = 315) kg m⁻³\nT0 = 273.15 K\n\nThe surface-energy defaults (SemixSurfaceEnergyBalance(), DynamicAlbedo() with alpha_dry, alpha_wet, alpha_ice = 0.81, 0.70, 0.40, CloudProxyLongwave(), SemixTurbulentFluxes() with sensible heat factor 3.0 and stable coefficient 40, DiurnalSubsteps() with 8 substeps and a 1 K temperature cycle) were calibrated against MAR v3.14.3 over Greenland with daily forcing. All values are listed on Parameters.","title":"Important Defaults"},{"category":"section","location":"model_state/#Entry-Points","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"Most users run a Simulation with run! (see Running Simulations). Coupled workflows advance it with time_step! or yearly_step! and change the inputs in between (see Models And Coupling). integrate!(model, steps) steps a model without a Simulation. Every path ends in column_step!, which advances one column by one forcing step and is the function inlined into the GPU kernel.","title":"Entry Points"},{"category":"type","location":"model_state/#Chion.BESSIState","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"struct BESSIState{NI<:AbstractVector{<:Integer}, MT<:AbstractMatrix, VT<:AbstractVector}\n\nPrognostic state of a BESSIModel: the snow and firn layers (layer, column) (on a GPU stored (column, layer) for coalesced access, see TransposedLayerMatrix), the ice-substrate temperatures, and per column the number of active layers, the cumulative mass fluxes (kg m⁻²), the time-integrated surface energy balance (W m⁻² day, positive into the surface) and the surface properties.\n\nFields\n\nN::AbstractVector{<:Integer}: number of active snow and firn layers\nmass::AbstractMatrix: solid mass of the layers (kg m⁻²)\nmass_w::AbstractMatrix: liquid water of the layers (kg m⁻²)\ndensity::AbstractMatrix: density of the layers (kg m⁻³)\ntemperature::AbstractMatrix: temperature of the layers (K)\nice_temperature::AbstractMatrix: temperature of the ice-substrate layers (K)\nmass_base::AbstractVector: firn mass passed to the ice below (kg m⁻², cumulative)\nsmb_ice::AbstractVector: net mass forcing of the ice sheet (kg m⁻², cumulative)\nrunoff::AbstractVector: runoff (kg m⁻², cumulative)\nmelt::AbstractVector: melt (kg m⁻², cumulative)\nrefreezing::AbstractVector: refreezing (kg m⁻², cumulative)\nvapor_mass::AbstractVector: net vapour exchange, deposition positive (kg m⁻², cumulative)\nsublimation::AbstractVector: sublimation (kg m⁻², cumulative)\nlatent_heat_flux_sum::AbstractVector: latent heat flux (W m⁻² day, cumulative)\nshortwave_down_sum::AbstractVector: shortwave down (W m⁻² day, cumulative)\nshortwave_net_sum::AbstractVector: absorbed shortwave (W m⁻² day, cumulative)\nlongwave_down_sum::AbstractVector: longwave down (W m⁻² day, cumulative)\nlongwave_net_sum::AbstractVector: net longwave (W m⁻² day, cumulative)\nsensible_heat_flux_sum::AbstractVector: sensible heat flux (W m⁻² day, cumulative)\nrain_heat_flux_sum::AbstractVector: heat of rain (W m⁻² day, cumulative)\nTsrf::AbstractVector: surface temperature (K)\nalbedo::AbstractVector: surface albedo (–)\nsnow_age_days::AbstractVector: time since the latest snowfall, AgingAlbedo (day)\nw_snow_max::AbstractVector: seasonal maximum of the snow water equivalent, SemixAlbedo (kg m⁻²)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.BESSIState"},{"category":"type","location":"model_state/#Chion.PDDState","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"struct PDDState{ST<:AbstractVector{<:Real}}\n\nState of a PDDModel: the snow reservoir and the cumulative mass fluxes (mm w.e.).\n\nFields\n\nsnowpack_swe::AbstractVector{<:Real}: snow water equivalent (mm w.e.)\nsmb_ice::AbstractVector{<:Real}: net mass forcing of the ice sheet (mm w.e., cumulative)\nrunoff::AbstractVector{<:Real}: runoff (mm w.e., cumulative)\npdd_sum::AbstractVector{<:Real}: positive degree days (K day, cumulative)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.PDDState"},{"category":"type","location":"model_state/#Chion.ITMState","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"struct ITMState{ST<:AbstractVector{<:Real}}\n\nState of an ITMModel. The instantaneous budget fields are in ITM's native mm w.e. day⁻¹; the cumulative fields (*_cum, smb_ice) in mm w.e.\n\nFields\n\nH_snow::AbstractVector{<:Real}: snow water equivalent (mm w.e.)\nalb_s::AbstractVector{<:Real}: surface albedo (–)\nsmb::AbstractVector{<:Real}: surface mass balance (mm w.e. day⁻¹)\nsmbi::AbstractVector{<:Real}: ice-sheet mass balance (mm w.e. day⁻¹)\nmelt::AbstractVector{<:Real}: melt (mm w.e. day⁻¹)\nrunoff::AbstractVector{<:Real}: runoff (mm w.e. day⁻¹)\nrefreezing::AbstractVector{<:Real}: refreezing (mm w.e. day⁻¹)\nTsrf::AbstractVector{<:Real}: surface temperature (K)\nmelt_net::AbstractVector{<:Real}: net melt (mm w.e. day⁻¹)\nsmb_cum::AbstractVector{<:Real}: surface mass balance (mm w.e., cumulative)\nsmb_ice::AbstractVector{<:Real}: ice-sheet mass balance (mm w.e., cumulative)\nmelt_cum::AbstractVector{<:Real}: melt (mm w.e., cumulative)\nrunoff_cum::AbstractVector{<:Real}: runoff (mm w.e., cumulative)\nrefreezing_cum::AbstractVector{<:Real}: refreezing (mm w.e., cumulative)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ITMState"},{"category":"function","location":"model_state/#Chion.initial_state","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"initial_state(grid, params) -> BESSIState | PDDState | ITMState\n\nInitial state of the columns of grid on the CPU: no snow, the initial density and temperature of params (a Params), or the initial bulk state for PDDParams and ITMParams.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.initial_state"},{"category":"type","location":"model_state/#Chion.BESSICache","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"mutable struct BESSICache{W<:Chion.ColumnarStepWorkspace, V<:AbstractVector, A<:AbstractVector{Int64}}\n\nDiagnostic and scratch arrays of a BESSIModel and the columns that are stepped.\n\nFields\n\nworkspace::Chion.ColumnarStepWorkspace: scratch of the column step (heat solve) and the cumulative boundary_enthalpy (J m⁻²)\nthickness::AbstractVector: snow and firn depth (m), from update_diagnostics!\nwet_mass::AbstractVector: solid plus liquid mass of the snow and firn (kg m⁻²), from update_diagnostics!\nbulk_density::AbstractVector: bulk density of the snow and firn (kg m⁻³), from update_diagnostics!\nliquid_water::AbstractVector: liquid water in the snow and firn (kg m⁻²), from update_diagnostics!\nactive::Vector{Bool}: stepped columns (host)\nactive_indices::AbstractVector{Int64}: indices of the stepped columns, on the model's backend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.BESSICache"},{"category":"type","location":"model_state/#Chion.BulkCache","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"mutable struct BulkCache{A<:AbstractVector{Int64}}\n\nThe columns that a bulk model (PDDModel, ITMModel) steps.\n\nFields\n\nactive::Vector{Bool}: stepped columns (host)\nactive_indices::AbstractVector{Int64}: indices of the stepped columns, on the model's backend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.BulkCache"},{"category":"type","location":"model_state/#Chion.AtmosphericForcing","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"mutable struct AtmosphericForcing{S<:Forcing}\n\nThe forcing of a model: its Forcing source and the loaded calendar year, as the host SnowpackForcing data and the kernel view fields on the model's backend in the model's number type. update_forcing! loads another year.\n\nFields\n\nsource::Forcing: where the forcing comes from, one calendar year at a time\nyear::Int64: the loaded calendar year\ndata::SnowpackForcing: the loaded year on the host\nfields::Union{Chion.ITMForcing, Chion.PDDForcing, NamedTuple}: the loaded year as the kernels read it, on the model's backend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AtmosphericForcing"},{"category":"function","location":"model_state/#Chion.update_diagnostics!","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"update_diagnostics!(model::BESSIModel)\n\nRecompute the column diagnostics thickness, wet_mass, bulk_density and liquid_water of the cache from the layers. run! calls it at the end of every run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.update_diagnostics!"},{"category":"function","location":"model_state/#Chion.column_profile","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"column_profile(model::BESSIModel, idx = 1) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe active layers of column idx (host copies): N, mass, mass_w, density, temperature, thickness (m), and the column's albedo, Tsrf and smb_ice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.column_profile"},{"category":"function","location":"model_state/#Chion.column_enthalpy","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"column_enthalpy(model, idx)\n\nEnthalpy (J m⁻²) of the snow/firn column and the ice substrate of column idx, relative to liquid water at T₀: solid mass contributes cᵢ m (T − T₀) − Lₘ m, liquid water (held at T₀) contributes nothing, and the fixed-mass substrate contributes its sensible heat only. Its change over a step equals the change of the workspace diagnostic boundary_enthalpy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.column_enthalpy"},{"category":"function","location":"model_state/#Chion.column_step!","page":"Model State And Step Flow","text":"column_step!(fields, params, layering, boundary, idx, forcing, workspace)\n\nAdvance column idx of the state arrays fields (get_fields(model.state)) by one forcing step with the step forcing forcing, the Params params, the Layering layering and the scratch arrays workspace. Mutates fields and workspace in place. The diurnal cycle of params selects one call of column_step_core! or up to max_substeps of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.column_step!"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Models-And-Coupling","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"For runs from forcing files with spin-up and outputs, see Running Simulations. This page describes the pieces underneath a Simulation, for forcing held in memory, step-by-step coupling to other models, GPUs and automatic differentiation:\n\nbuild a Grid: the columns, their optional x–y layout and the vertical Layering\nbuild the forcing: a file-backed Forcing or an in-memory SnowpackForcing (see its docstring below for the optional fields)\nbuild BESSIModel, PDDModel or ITMModel with its parameters (Params, PDDParams, ITMParams)\nwrap it in a Simulation and call run!, or step the model directly\n\njulia> using Chion\n\njulia> grid = Grid(2; layering = Layering(Ntot = 20));\n\njulia> forcing = SnowpackForcing(dt_days = ones(3), ncol = 2, air_temperature_c = -5.0,\n           snowfall_mm_day = 2.0, rainfall_mm_day = 0.0, shortwave_down = 100.0, latitude_deg = 70.0);\n\njulia> model = BESSIModel(grid; forcing, params = Params(albedo_scheme = AgingAlbedo()));\n\njulia> sim = Simulation(model; verbose = false);\n\njulia> run!(sim);\n\njulia> finished(sim), model.Ntot, size(model.mass)\n(true, 20, (20, 2))\n\nA model holds its grid, the prognostic state, a cache (diagnostics, scratch arrays and the active columns), the params and the forcing (the source and the loaded calendar year). Their fields are forwarded, so model.mass and model.Tsrf read the state, model.thickness the cache, model.alpha_dry and model.albedo_scheme the parameters, model.ncol and model.Ntot the grid, and model.year the loaded forcing year. A model knows nothing about time beyond the loaded forcing year; a Simulation advances it.\n\nBESSIModel resolves the layered mass and energy balance. Its default parameters are the setup calibrated against MAR over Greenland for daily forcing (see Parameters). The default diurnal substeps require latitude_deg in the forcing; use Params(diurnal_cycle = NoDiurnalCycle()) when latitude is unavailable or the forcing is already sub-daily. PDDModel uses a bulk snow reservoir with the degree-day factors ddf_snow and ddf_ice, refreezing_fraction, temperature_sigma, H_snow_max and the method of PDDParams. The default method = SimplePDD() uses the positive mean temperature; method = PISMPDD() applies the Calov–Greve expectation integral at every timestep.\n\nITMModel ports the Fortran insolation–temperature–melt scheme. It requires explicit latitude_deg, surface_height, ice_thickness (m), and annual_pdd (K day) in SnowpackForcing. q_sw_net, when supplied, is its insolation driver; otherwise ITM uses shortwave_down. As in the original ITM, this insolation is top-of-atmosphere radiation that the model attenuates with its elevation-dependent transmissivity; supplying surface shortwave (for example MAR SWD) attenuates it twice and strongly underestimates melt. It preserves the Fortran scheme's per-step mmWE day^-1 fields (smb, smbi, melt, runoff, refreezing) and cumulative mmWE fields.","title":"Models And Coupling"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Number-Type","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"NF sets the floating-point type of the grid, the parameters, the state and the forcing fields that the kernels read. The grid and the parameters must agree: Grid(n; NF = Float32) with Params(; NF = Float32), or BESSIModel(forcing; NF = Float32), which builds both. PDDModel and ITMModel take PDDParams(; NF) and ITMParams(; NF) in the same way.","title":"Number Type"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Backends","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"A model lives on a KernelAbstractions backend: CPU() (multithreaded, the default) or, after using CUDA, CUDABackend(). CUDA.jl is a weak dependency: using CUDA loads the GPU backend (the ChionCUDAExt package extension).\n\nusing CUDA\nmodel = BESSIModel(grid; forcing, backend = CUDABackend())\nmodel = to_backend(model, CUDABackend())     # or move an existing model\nstate = cpu_state(model)                     # host copy of the state\nset_state!(model, state)                     # copy a host state (e.g. a restart) back\n\nReactantBackend() (after using Reactant, CUDA, the ChionReactantExt extension) runs a BESSIModel through Reactant.jl and XLA: the column step is compiled once per integrate! call or forcing year and stepped from the host, and agrees with the CPU to roundoff (test/reactant/runtests.jl, its own environment). PDD and ITM models, resetting columns and device-side output accumulation are not available there.","title":"Backends"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Coupling","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"A Simulation can be advanced one forcing step or one year at a time, with the model's inputs changed in between:\n\nsim = Simulation(model; years = 2000:2100)\ntime_step!(sim)                              # one forcing step (spin-up first, if any)\nset_input!(sim, :surface_height, z_s)        # e.g. from an ice-sheet model, per column\nset_active_mask!(sim, ice_mask)              # step only these columns\nfields = yearly_step!(sim)                   # the next forcing year (must start at its first step)\nfields.smb_ice_yearly                        # kg m⁻² per column; fields.mean_T_srf_K in K\nrun!(sim; years = 10)                        # ten more years\n\nset_input! replaces a forcing field of the loaded year (after editing model.forcing.data in place, sync_forcing!(model) moves it to the kernels); set_active_mask! takes a column vector or a y–x matrix on the grid, and resets columns that become inactive to the initial state unless reset_newly_inactive = false. yearly_step! returns the annual coupling fields smb_ice_yearly and mean_T_srf_K, also as y–x matrices (*_grid) when the grid has spatial coordinates.\n\nWithout a Simulation, integrate!(model, steps) advances the model over the forcing steps steps of the loaded year, without output and without changing the year; update_forcing!(model, year) loads another year. integrate! is the entry point for automatic differentiation.","title":"Coupling"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Automatic-Differentiation","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"Columns are smooth functions of the parameters and the forcing between discrete events (layer split/merge, snow↔ice switches), so derivatives are pathwise slopes. Two paths are tested against finite differences (test/test_autodiff.jl):\n\nForwardDiff through a whole model: build it at a dual number type and give the parameter of interest a unit partial (using ForwardDiff loads ChionForwardDiffExt, which lets outputs and logs take the values of the duals):\n\nusing Chion, ForwardDiff\nfunction melt(alpha_dry)\n    NF = typeof(alpha_dry)\n    model = BESSIModel(Grid(n; NF); forcing, params = Params(; NF, alpha_dry))\n    integrate!(model, 1:365)\n    return sum(model.state.melt)\nend\nForwardDiff.derivative(melt, 0.81)          # kg m⁻² per unit albedo\n\nEnzyme forward mode through column_step!, on the CPU and inside the GPU kernel (autodiff_deferred), with the state arrays as Duplicated arguments.\n\nScheme coefficients differentiate the same way (SemixTurbulentFluxes(sensible_exchange_factor = Dual(…))). Reverse mode is not available for the layered model.","title":"Automatic Differentiation"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Input-Units","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"SnowpackForcing accepts either model-native fields or user-facing fields:\n\nair_temperature, snowfall_rate, and rainfall_rate use native units (K, kg m^-2 s^-1, kg m^-2 s^-1).\nair_temperature_c, snowfall_mm_day, and rainfall_mm_day are converted to native units.\nshortwave_down is always W m^-2.\nice_thickness is metres and annual_pdd is K day; both are required by ITMModel.\nscalar and time-vector forcing values are broadcast over columns.\n\nA model takes a SnowpackForcing directly (it becomes a one-year Forcing). load_forcing_file can select spatial columns directly with mask_name and mask_threshold. Selection depends only on that mask variable. Each field is read once straight into the selected columns; dimensions are recognised by the names given as x_name/y_name (or x/y) and a time dimension, and any other dimension (vertical level, sector) is read at its first index. read_forcing_columns exposes the same reader for additional variables.","title":"Input Units"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Host-Forcing-Helpers","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"A host whose climate supplies only monthly air temperature and precipitation can derive the rest with shortwave_down_from_transmissivity (shortwave from a per-domain direct transmissivity; the longwave cloud proxy then diagnoses its own cloudiness), wind_speed_from_geometry (wind from elevation and slope), temperature_variability_sigma and red_noise_monthly (daily temperature variability about a smooth series). Chion does not call them itself. clear_sky_transmissivity needs the parameters of a model, whose CloudProxyLongwave holds the coefficients:\n\ntau_cs = clear_sky_transmissivity(model.params, 2000.0, 300.0)   # height (m), TOA SW (W m⁻²)","title":"Host Forcing Helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Reference","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#Chion.Grid","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"struct Grid{NF, L<:Union{Nothing, SpatialLayout}}\n\nThe discretisation of a model: ncol independent columns, their optional x–y layout, and the vertical Layering of every column, at the floating-point type NF of the model.\n\nGrid(ncol; NF = Float64, layering = Layering(), x, y, js, is, mask)\nGrid(forcing::Forcing; NF = Float64, layering = Layering())\n\nx, y, js, is (and optionally mask) give the SpatialLayout; without them the columns have no coordinates. Grid(forcing) takes the columns and layout of a file-backed forcing.\n\njulia> grid = Grid(4; layering = Layering(Ntot = 20));\n\njulia> grid.Ntot, grid.ncol\n(20, 4)\n\nFields\n\nncol::Int64: number of columns\nlayout::Union{Nothing, SpatialLayout}: x–y positions of the columns, or nothing\nlayering::Layering{NF} where NF: vertical discretisation of every column\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Grid"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#Chion.Layering","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"struct Layering{NF<:Real}\n\nVertical discretisation of a column: up to Ntot snow and firn layers whose masses are kept between mass_min and mass_max (a layer above mass_max splits into mass_split and the rest), finer layers near the surface (near_surface_layer_max_thicknesses_m, the maximum thicknesses of the top four layers), and a thermal ice substrate of ice_substrate_layers layers below the firn whose thicknesses double downward from ice_substrate_top_thickness_m.\n\nFields\n\nNtot::Int64 = DEFAULT_NTOT: maximum number of snow and firn layers (default 15)\nmass_max::Real = DEFAULT_MASS_MAX: mass above which a layer splits (kg m⁻², default 500)\nmass_split::Real = DEFAULT_MASS_SPLIT: mass of the upper part of a split layer (kg m⁻², default 300)\nmass_min::Real = DEFAULT_MASS_MIN: mass below which a layer merges with its neighbour (kg m⁻², default 100)\nnear_surface_layer_max_thicknesses_m::NTuple{4, NF} where NF<:Real = (0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.3): maximum thicknesses of the top four layers (m, default (0.02, 0.05, 0.10, 0.30))\nice_substrate_layers::Int64 = 5: number of ice-substrate layers below the firn (default 5; 0 for snow on land)\nice_substrate_top_thickness_m::Real = 0.05: thickness of the top ice-substrate layer (m, default 0.05)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Layering"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#Chion.SpatialLayout","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"struct SpatialLayout\n\nPositions of the columns on a regular x–y grid: column k is the cell (js[k], is[k]) of the (length(y), length(x)) matrix mask.\n\nFields\n\nx::Vector{Float64}: x coordinates of the grid (m or degrees)\ny::Vector{Float64}: y coordinates of the grid\njs::Vector{Int64}: row (y index) of every column\nis::Vector{Int64}: column (x index) of every column\nmask::Matrix{Float64}: mask of the grid cells (1 where a column is)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.SpatialLayout"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#Chion.BESSIModel","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"mutable struct BESSIModel{G<:Grid, S<:BESSIState, C<:Chion.BESSICache, P<:Params, B<:BoundaryConditions, F<:Chion.AtmosphericForcing} <: Chion.AbstractSnowModel\n\nThe layered BESSI snowpack and firn model: a Grid, the BESSIState, the BESSICache, the Params and the AtmosphericForcing. Wrap it in a Simulation to run it.\n\nBESSIModel(grid; forcing, params = Params(), boundary = BoundaryConditions(), backend = CPU(), state = nothing)\n\nforcing is a Forcing or an in-memory SnowpackForcing; its first year is loaded. backend is a KernelAbstractions backend (CPU(), CUDABackend()) or its name (:threads, :gpu). state (a host BESSIState, e.g. a restart) replaces the initial state of params. Fields resolve through the model: model.mass (state), model.thickness (cache), model.alpha_dry (params), model.ncol, model.Ntot (grid), model.year (loaded forcing year).\n\njulia> grid = Grid(2);\n\njulia> forcing = SnowpackForcing(dt_days = ones(3), ncol = 2, air_temperature_c = -5.0, snowfall_mm_day = 2.0,\n           rainfall_mm_day = 0.0, shortwave_down = 100.0, latitude_deg = 70.0);\n\njulia> model = BESSIModel(grid; forcing, params = Params(albedo_scheme = ConstantAlbedo()));\n\njulia> model.albedo_scheme, size(model.mass)\n(ConstantAlbedo(), (15, 2))\n\nFields\n\ngrid::Grid: columns, layout and layering\nstate::BESSIState: prognostic arrays\ncache::Chion.BESSICache: diagnostics, scratch and active columns\nparams::Params: physical constants and schemes\nboundary::BoundaryConditions: surface and bottom boundary conditions of the heat solve\nforcing::Chion.AtmosphericForcing: the forcing source and the loaded year\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.BESSIModel"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#Chion.PDDModel","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"mutable struct PDDModel{G<:Grid, S<:PDDState, C<:Chion.BulkCache, P<:PDDParams, F<:Chion.AtmosphericForcing} <: Chion.AbstractSnowModel\n\nBulk positive-degree-day surface mass balance: a one-layer snow reservoir capped at H_snow_max, melt from the PDDParams.\n\nPDDModel(grid; forcing, params = PDDParams(), backend = CPU(), state = nothing)\n\nFields\n\ngrid::Grid: columns and layout\nstate::PDDState: prognostic arrays\ncache::Chion.BulkCache: active columns\nparams::PDDParams: degree-day factors and constants\nforcing::Chion.AtmosphericForcing: the forcing source and the loaded year\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.PDDModel"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#Chion.ITMModel","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"mutable struct ITMModel{G<:Grid, S<:ITMState, C<:Chion.BulkCache, P<:ITMParams, F<:Chion.AtmosphericForcing} <: Chion.AbstractSnowModel\n\nBulk insolation–temperature–melt surface mass balance, ported from the Fortran fesmc/chion/smbpal code. Needs surface_height, ice_thickness, annual_pdd and latitude_deg in the forcing; q_sw_net is the insolation driver where it is given, otherwise shortwave_down.\n\nITMModel(grid; forcing, params = ITMParams(), backend = CPU(), state = nothing)\n\nFields\n\ngrid::Grid: columns and layout\nstate::ITMState: prognostic arrays\ncache::Chion.BulkCache: active columns\nparams::ITMParams: ITM coefficients and constants\nforcing::Chion.AtmosphericForcing: the forcing source and the loaded year\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ITMModel"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.to_backend","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"to_backend(state, backend)\n\nA copy of state with every array on the KernelAbstractions backend (CPU(), or e.g. CUDABackend() after using CUDA).\n\n\n\n\n\nto_backend(model, backend)\n\nA new model with the state, cache and loaded forcing on the KernelAbstractions backend (CPU(), or CUDABackend() after using CUDA); the original is not modified. The grid, the parameters and the forcing source are shared.\n\nusing CUDA\nmodel = to_backend(BESSIModel(grid; forcing), CUDABackend())\n\n\n\n\n\nto_backend(sim, backend)\n\nA simulation of to_backend(sim.model, backend) with the same years, spin-up and outputs, before it has started.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.to_backend"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#Chion.ReactantBackend","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"ReactantBackend()\n\nRun a BESSIModel through Reactant.jl (XLA) on the device Reactant targets: to_backend(model, ReactantBackend()) or BESSIModel(grid; forcing, backend = ReactantBackend()). Needs using Reactant, CUDA (the ChionReactantExt extension; CUDA.jl also on the CPU, since the kernels are compiled through it). The column step is compiled once per integrate! call or forcing year and stepped from the host; results agree with the other backends to roundoff. Not supported: PDD/ITM models, resetting columns with set_active_mask!, and outputs that accumulate on the device.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ReactantBackend"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.cpu_state","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"cpu_state(model) -> BESSIState | PDDState | ITMState\n\nA copy of the model's state on the CPU (layers (layer, column)), e.g. to inspect or save the state of a GPU run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.cpu_state"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.set_state!","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"set_state!(model, state)\n\nCopy the host state (e.g. from cpu_state or a restart) into the model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.set_state!"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.set_active_mask!","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"set_active_mask!(model, mask; reset_newly_inactive = true)\n\nStep only the columns where mask is true (a column vector or a y–x matrix on the grid). Columns that become inactive are reset to the initial state unless reset_newly_inactive = false.\n\n\n\n\n\nset_active_mask!(sim, mask; reset_newly_inactive = true)\n\nset_active_mask! of the simulation's model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.set_active_mask!"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.set_input!","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"set_input!(model, name, values)\n\nReplace the forcing field name (e.g. :surface_height from an ice-sheet model) of the loaded year by values: a column vector (constant in time) or a (column, time) matrix. The air pressure derived from the surface height is not recomputed.\n\n\n\n\n\nset_input!(sim, name, values)\n\nset_input! of the simulation's model, for the loaded forcing year.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.set_input!"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.sync_forcing!","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"sync_forcing!(model)\n\nRebuild the kernel view of the loaded forcing year from its host data, after the fields of model.forcing.data (a SnowpackForcing) were changed in place, e.g. by a coupled ice-sheet model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.sync_forcing!"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.update_forcing!","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"update_forcing!(model, year)\n\nLoad the calendar year year of the model's forcing source, unless it is loaded already.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.update_forcing!"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.integrate!","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"integrate!(model, steps)\n\nAdvance model over the forcing steps steps (a range of step indices of the loaded forcing year) without a Simulation: no output and no change of the forcing year. The entry point for automatic differentiation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.integrate!"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.yearly_step!","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"yearly_step!(sim::Simulation{<:BESSIModel}) -> NamedTuple\n\nRun the next forcing year of sim (which must be at the start of a year) and return the coupling fields of an ice-sheet model: smb_ice_yearly, the change of smb_ice over the year (kg m⁻², zero in inactive columns), and mean_T_srf_K, the time-mean surface temperature (K, NaN in inactive columns), as column vectors and, with a spatial grid, as y–x matrices (*_grid).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.yearly_step!"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#Chion.SnowpackForcing","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"SnowpackForcing(; dt_days, air_temperature_c, snowfall_mm_day, rainfall_mm_day,\n                shortwave_down, time_values=nothing, ncol=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nTime-varying atmospheric boundary conditions for a snowpack model, one value per column and forcing step. Fields are given as a matrix (ncol, ntime), a vector over time (one column, or the same for every column), or a scalar.\n\nRequired:\n\ndt_days: length of each forcing step (days); its length sets ntime.\nthe air temperature and precipitation, either as air_temperature_c (°C), snowfall_mm_day, rainfall_mm_day (mm w.e. day⁻¹) or in model units as air_temperature (K), snowfall_rate, rainfall_rate (kg m⁻² s⁻¹).\nshortwave_down (W m⁻²).\n\nOptional (missing fields use the parameterisations of the model):\n\ntime_values: DateTime of each step (default: daily from 2001-01-01 12:00); sets the calendar years and the solar geometry.\nlatitude_deg, surface_height (m): needed by the diurnal substeps and the cloud-proxy longwave; surface_height also gives the barometric air_pressure when no pressure is supplied.\nwind_speed (m s⁻¹, default 5), relative_humidity (fraction or %), air_pressure (Pa).\nprescribed surface fluxes q_sw_net, q_lw_down, q_sh, q_lh (W m⁻²), prescribed_albedo (with albedo=:prescribed), prescribed_melt (kg m⁻² per step). Each has an optional has_* mask to prescribe it only for some columns or steps.\nSEMIX albedo inputs cloud, coszm, dust_deposition, z_sur_std, prescribed_ice_albedo.\nice_thickness, annual_pdd: ITM inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.SnowpackForcing"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.update_air_pressure!","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"update_air_pressure!(forcing::SnowpackForcing; temperature_mode=:instantaneous, ...)\n\nOverwrite forcing.air_pressure with the barometric pressure diagnosed from surface_height and the air temperature (air_pressure_from_surface_height).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.update_air_pressure!"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.load_forcing_file","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"load_forcing_file(path; kwargs...) -> (grid, forcing)\n\nLoad a generic NetCDF forcing file into a Grid and SnowpackForcing. Required variables default to x, y, time, TT, SF, RF, and SWD; optional turbulent/radiative flux fields and prescribed surface albedo are loaded when present and requested. If no explicit pressure field is requested, the static surface_height_name field is used to derive air pressure with the barometric formula. Set mask_name and mask_threshold to load only columns selected by a spatial mask variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.load_forcing_file"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.read_forcing_columns","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"read_forcing_columns(ds, name, rows, ntime, ny, nx; x_name=\"x\", y_name=\"y\")\n\nRead the gridded variable name from the open dataset ds as a (length(rows), ntime) Float64 matrix in one pass. rows are flat column indices i + (j - 1) * nx. The x and y dimensions are found by the names x_name/y_name (or x/y) and the time dimension by name; any other dimension (vertical level, sector) is read at its first index only. Variables without recognisable x/y/time dimensions fall back to shape matching.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.read_forcing_columns"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.clear_sky_transmissivity","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"clear_sky_transmissivity(params, height, toa)\n\nDaily shortwave transmissivity of a clear sky (Konzelmann et al., 1994) at the surface height height (m) and daily-mean top-of-atmosphere shortwave toa (W m⁻²), τcs = min(1, τ₀ + τz z + τS ln(SWTOA / 300 W m⁻²)) with z in km, the reference of the cloud proxy's cloud fraction. A height that is not finite (no surface height) or negative counts as sea level; toa is floored at 50.0 W m⁻², as in the proxy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.clear_sky_transmissivity"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.shortwave_down_from_transmissivity","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"shortwave_down_from_transmissivity(toa, height; trans0, trans_per_km)\n\nSurface shortwave down (W m⁻²) from the top-of-atmosphere shortwave toa (W m⁻²) and the surface height height (m) with a per-domain direct transmissivity: SW↓ = SWTOA clamp(τ₀ + τkm max(z, 0)[km], 0, 1). A height that is not finite counts as sea level. The cloud proxy then diagnoses its own cloudiness from this SW↓.\n\nCalibration (with Chion's TOA): MAR v3.14 1981-2010 over Greenland's ice gives τ₀ = 0.587, τkm = 0.052 km⁻¹ in summer; CERES gives τ₀ = 0.68, τkm = 0.041 km⁻¹ over Antarctica.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.shortwave_down_from_transmissivity"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.wind_speed_from_geometry","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"wind_speed_from_geometry(z_srf, dx; u0, u_z=0, u_s=0, slope_length_km=50)\n\nWind speed (m s⁻¹) from the surface elevation z_srf (m) on a regular grid of spacing dx (m): U = u0 + uz max(z, 0)[km] + us |∇z|[m/km], at least 0. The slope is a centred difference over 2n cells, n = max(1, round(L/(2 dx))) for the baseline L = slope_length_km, so that it does not depend on the grid's resolution; one-sided over the cells available at the edges. u_s = 0 needs no dx.\n\nCalibration: ERA5 1981-2010 summer 10-m wind over the ice gives Greenland 2.5 + 1.27 z[km], Antarctica 5.5 + 0.2 z[km] (u_s = 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.wind_speed_from_geometry"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.temperature_variability_sigma","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"temperature_variability_sigma(t_mon, T0; sigma0, slope, smin, smax)\n\nStandard deviation (K) of the daily air temperature about a smooth (monthly-interpolated) daily series, from the monthly mean air temperature t_mon (K): σ = clamp(σ₀ + slope (Tmon − T₀), smin, smax). The daily temperature is then T = Tsmooth + σ ξ, with ξ from red_noise_monthly. σ falls with the monthly mean temperature and is lowest over melting surfaces (Seguinot and Rogozhina 2014, J. Glaciol. 60, 603).\n\nCalibration: ERA5 1981-2010 over the Greenland and Antarctic ice gives σ = 2.0 − 0.14 (T_mon − T₀) in [2, 6] K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.temperature_variability_sigma"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#Chion.red_noise_monthly","page":"Models And Coupling","text":"red_noise_monthly(nday, dt_days, nday_mon, tau, seed)\n\nOne year of red noise ξ, a value per step of dt_days days (nday ÷ dt_days values): AR(1) with the lag-one correlation exp(−dt/τ), e-folding time tau (days; ERA5: 4-6 d), driven by standard normals; then its mean removed in every month of nday_mon days and the whole scaled to unit variance.\n\nThe normals come from a portable Park-Miller minimal standard generator, x ← 16807 x mod (2³¹ − 1) with x₀ = mod(|seed|, 2³¹ − 2) + 1, two uniforms per normal and the Box-Muller cosine branch, so a seed gives the same sequence as the Fortran chion's forcing_red_noise_monthly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.red_noise_monthly"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/percolation/#Percolation","page":"Percolation","text":"go_percolation! moves liquid water downward with a bucket scheme. Each layer retains water up to a volumetric content of max_lwc = 0.1 of its pore volume. The pass runs once from the surface downward, before refreezing, so water in excess of the holding capacity passes cold layers within the same step. Water leaving the deepest layer runs off, because the underlying ice is impermeable. Lateral runoff of water ponding on ice layers within the firn is not represented.","title":"Percolation"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/percolation/#Model-Formulation","page":"Percolation","text":"For each active layer i, the code first computes pore volume as\n\nphi_i =\nfracm_sirho_i^mathrmsnow\n- fracm_sirho_i^mathrmice\n\nIf phi_i  0, liquid-water content is\n\nmathrmLWC_i =\nfracm_wirho_w phi_i\n\nIf mathrmLWC_i  mathrmLWC_max, the excess liquid water is\n\nDelta m_wi =\nleft(mathrmLWC_i - mathrmLWC_maxright)rho_wphi_i\n\nand is routed to the next active layer or to runoff.","title":"Model Formulation"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/percolation/#Pore-Collapse-Routing","page":"Percolation","text":"If the pore volume is nonpositive, the current implementation routes all liquid water in that layer downward immediately, or to runoff if there is no deeper active snow layer.\n\nLayers with zero solid mass cannot retain water: their liquid water goes directly to runoff.","title":"Pore-Collapse Routing"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/percolation/#Notes","page":"Percolation","text":"max_lwc = 0.1 is fixed inside the column step; it is a keyword only of the standalone go_percolation!\nreturned runoff has units mathrmkgm^-2","title":"Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/percolation/#API","page":"Percolation","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/percolation/#Chion.go_percolation!","page":"Percolation","text":"go_percolation!(solid_mass, liquid_water_mass, snow_density, ice_density, water_density; ...)\n\nRun the percolation scheme on one vector-backed snow column. Mutates liquid_water_mass in-place and returns the runoff mass.\n\n\n\n\n\ngo_percolation!(model, idx; max_lwc = 0.1)\n\nRun liquid-water percolation for column idx of a CPU model. Mutates mass_w and accumulates the routed runoff into runoff[idx] of its state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.go_percolation!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/percolation/#Chion._go_percolation!-processes-percolation","page":"Percolation","text":"_go_percolation!(N_storage, mass, mass_w, density, idx, ice_density, water_density; max_lwc=0.1)\n\nRoute excess liquid water downward through column idx until each layer is at or below the liquid-water-content threshold. Mutates mass_w in-place and returns runoff leaving the bottom of the column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._go_percolation!"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/densification/#Densification","page":"Densification","text":"go_densification! updates layer density. The low-density scheme is chosen with the densification_scheme slot of Params (BESSIDensification(), the default, or HTESSELDensification()); firn above 550 kg m⁻³ always follows Barnola et al. (1991).","title":"Densification"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/densification/#Common-Update-Rule","page":"Densification","text":"For each active layer, the code computes a density tendency dotrho and then applies\n\nrho^n+1 =\nminleft(\nrho_i\nmaxleft(rho^n rho^n + dotrhoDelta tright)\nright)\n\nThe update is nondecreasing and capped at ice density rho_i.\n\nThe overburden pressure used by the densification laws is\n\nsigma = gleft(M_mathrmabove + fracm2right)\n\nwith g = 9.81 m s^-2.","title":"Common Update Rule"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/densification/#Low-Density-Branch-(rho-550-kg-m-3)","page":"Densification","text":"","title":"Low-Density Branch (rho < 550 kg m^-3)"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/densification/#BESSI-Option-(BESSIDensification)","page":"Densification","text":"The BESSI-style tendency is\n\ndotrho =\n0011\nexpleft(-frac101608314Tright)\n(rho_i - rho)max(A_t 0)\n\nafter Herron and Langway (1980), where A_t = max(P_mathrmsnow 0) + P_mathrmrain is the accumulation rate in kg m⁻² s⁻¹ of the current step and T the layer temperature. Snow below 550 kg m⁻³ therefore densifies only in steps with precipitation.","title":"BESSI Option (BESSIDensification)"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/densification/#HTESSEL-Option-(HTESSELDensification)","page":"Densification","text":"The HTESSEL-style tendency is\n\ndotrho = rho left(fracsigmaeta + xi right)\n\nwith\n\neta = 37times 10^7\nexpleft(81times 10^-2(T_0 - T) + 18times 10^-2rhoright)\n\nand\n\nxi = 28times 10^-6\nexpleft(-42times 10^-2(T_0 - T) - 460max(0rho - 150)right)\n\nWhen HTESSEL is active, retained liquid water can also compact low-density snow after the energy, melt, and percolation updates:\n\nrho^new =\nminleft(\nrho_i\nmaxleft(rho rho + rhofracDelta m_wmright)\nright)\nqquad\nDelta m_w = maxleft(m_w^after - m_w^before 0right)\n\nHere m_w^before is the liquid water saved before the energy solve, and the compaction applies only to layers below 550 kg m⁻³.","title":"HTESSEL Option (HTESSELDensification)"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/densification/#Intermediate-Density-Branch-(550-rho-800-kg-m-3)","page":"Densification","text":"The code uses\n\ndotrho =\n25400\nexpleft(-frac600008314Tright)\nrhof(rho)(Delta P)^3\n\nwith\n\nf(rho)=10^-29166(rhorho_i)^3 + 84422(rhorho_i)^2 - 87425(rhorho_i) + 30673\n\nThe effective pressure is\n\nDelta P = P_mathrmice - P_mathrmbubble\n\nwhere\n\nP_mathrmice = frac981(M_mathrmabove + m2)10^6\n\nand\n\nP_mathrmbubble=\nfracP_mathrmatm10^6\nleft(\nfrac(1rho_e-1rho_i)(1rho-1rho_i)-1\nright)\n\nThe current implementation uses rho_e = 815 kg m^-3 and P_atm = 101325 Pa internally. P_mathrmbubble is zero for rho le rho_e (no closed pores), so in practice it acts only in the high-density branch below.","title":"Intermediate-Density Branch (550 <= rho < 800 kg m^-3)"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/densification/#High-Density-Branch-(rho-800-kg-m-3)","page":"Densification","text":"The same Arrhenius prefactor is used with the alternative shape factor\n\nf(rho)=frac316\nfrac1-rhorho_i\nleft(1-(1-rhorho_i)^13right)^3","title":"High-Density Branch (rho >= 800 kg m^-3)"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/densification/#API","page":"Densification","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/densification/#Chion.go_densification!","page":"Densification","text":"go_densification!(model, idx, accumulation_rate, dt_seconds)\n\nAdvance the snow density of column idx of a CPU model over one time step. Mutates its density in place and returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.go_densification!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/densification/#Chion._htessel_low_density_rate-processes-densification","page":"Densification","text":"_htessel_low_density_rate(c, overburden_pressure, temperature, density)\n\nReturn the HTESSEL low-density densification tendency for one layer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._htessel_low_density_rate"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/densification/#Chion._apply_htessel_liquid_water_compaction!-processes-densification","page":"Densification","text":"_apply_htessel_liquid_water_compaction!(N_storage, mass, mass_w, density, idx, liquid_water_before_energy, ice_density)\n\nApply the HTESSEL liquid-water compaction correction after percolation and energy updates. Mutates density in-place for low-density layers that retain additional liquid water.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._apply_htessel_liquid_water_compaction!"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Accumulation-And-Melt","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"Accumulation and melt are handled by the internal helpers _apply_accumulation_resolved! and _melt_surface_layers!, which are called from column_step!. They are documented here because they define the model behavior, but the intended public entry points are run! and integrate!.","title":"Accumulation And Melt"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Accumulation","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"With snowfall rate P_mathrmsnow and rainfall rate P_mathrmrain in mathrmkgm^-2s^-1 and timestep Delta t in seconds, the added masses are\n\nDelta m_mathrmsnow = P_mathrmsnowDelta t\nqquad\nDelta m_mathrmrain = P_mathrmrainDelta t\n\nIf the column is empty, a new surface layer is created only when snowfall is positive. Rain alone does not create a snow layer, and rain on bare ice (or on a remnant layer below a trace threshold) runs off. Snow falling on a previously bare surface takes min(T_mathrmair T_0) in every new layer.","title":"Accumulation"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Fresh-Snow-Density","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"The scheme is selected with the fresh_snow_density_scheme slot of Params. With ConstantFreshSnowDensity() (the default), the code uses its density (315 kg m⁻³) clamped to [50, rho_i].\n\nWith ParameterizedFreshSnowDensity(), the code uses the HTESSEL formulation (Dutra et al., 2010)\n\nrho_mathrmfresh = a + b(T_mathrmair - T_0) + csqrtV\n\nwith default coefficients intercept = 109 kg m⁻³, temperature_coefficient = 6 kg m⁻³ K⁻¹ and wind_coefficient = 26 kg m⁻⁷ᐟ² s¹ᐟ², wind speed V in m s⁻¹ (5 m s⁻¹ without wind forcing), and a final clamp to 50 rho_i.\n\n(Image: Fresh-snow density vs wind speed)\n\nIf snowfall is added to an existing surface layer, the new bulk density is mixed by conserving layer volume:\n\nrho_1^new =\nfracm_1^old + Delta m_mathrmsnow\nm_1^oldrho_1^old + Delta m_mathrmsnowrho_mathrmfresh\n\nRainfall is added directly to the surface liquid-water store mass_w[1] when a snow layer exists.","title":"Fresh-Snow Density"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Layer-Structure-Enforcement","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"After mass addition, the code:\n\nsplits the surface layer when mass[1] > mass_max\nfrees space at the bottom when all Ntot layers are already active, by merging the two deepest layers and exporting any density excess above rho_i to the ice\nmerges or rebalances the surface when mass[1] < mass_min; with near-surface layer limits (the default) this minimum is not applied, because the remeshing controls the surface layers\napplies the snow-depth cap described on the Layer Structure And Basal Transfer page\nremeshes the four uppermost layers to near_surface_layer_max_thicknesses_m (see Near-Surface Layers)","title":"Layer-Structure Enforcement"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Melt","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"If the energy solve holds the surface at the melting point, the energy available for melt is the difference between the atmospheric flux at T_s = T_0 and the heat conducted into the uppermost layer,\n\nE_mathrmmelt = Delta tleftQ_mathrmnet(T_0) - G_sleft(T_0 - T_1^n+1right)right\n\n(see Energy Balance). _melt_surface_layers! consumes this energy from the surface downward. Because a melting layer can be colder than T_0, each kilogram costs L_m + c_i (T_0 - T_i) to warm and melt, so the melt mass of layer i is EL_m + c_i (T_0 - T_i). The melted mass is added to the layer's liquid water, and the later retention and refreezing decisions are left to go_percolation! and go_refreezing!.\n\nlayers that melt completely are removed, and their liquid water is passed to the layer below\nmelt energy left after all snow is gone melts the underlying ice: it lowers smb_ice and runs off directly\nafter melt, the interface temperature Tsrf is set to T_0\n\nIf melt is prescribed (prescribed_melt forcing), the melt mass is taken directly from the forcing and removed by _apply_melt! at latent heat only. On bare ice, melt follows from the same energy balance with the top ice-substrate layer as the surface (see Layer Structure).","title":"Melt"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Interaction-In-The-Column-Step","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"The accumulation-and-melt-related ordering in one call to column_step! is:\n\naccumulation, removal of trace layers, and near-surface remeshing (or the bare-ice branch if no snow is left)\nalbedo update\ndensification\ngo_energy_flux!\nvapour exchange (sublimation/deposition) on the surface layer\nmelt when melt energy is available, or the prescribed melt when prescribed_melt forcing is supplied\ngo_percolation!\nHTESSEL liquid-water compaction when enabled\ngo_refreezing!\nnear-surface remeshing and the final albedo update\n\nThe full sequence is listed on Model State And Step Flow.","title":"Interaction In The Column Step"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Internal-Reference","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"","title":"Internal Reference"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Chion._fresh_snow_density-processes-accumulation_ablation","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"_fresh_snow_density(c, air_temperature, wind_speed)\n\nDiagnose the density of newly fallen snow from the configured scheme in c. The result is clamped to a physically plausible range between fresh snow and ice density.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._fresh_snow_density"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Chion._apply_accumulation_resolved!-processes-accumulation_ablation","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"_apply_accumulation!(..., snowfall_rate, rainfall_rate, dt_seconds; air_temperature=nothing, wind_speed=...)\n\nApply snowfall and rainfall to column idx, update the surface layer, and maintain layer-structure constraints such as split and merge thresholds. Mutates the supplied state arrays in-place and may also update albedo, runoff, and basal-mass diagnostics. Returns the enthalpy of the ice exported to the ice reservoir (relative to liquid water at T₀).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._apply_accumulation_resolved!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Chion._melt_surface_layers!-processes-accumulation_ablation","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"_melt_surface_layers!(..., idx, mass_split, mass_min, melt_mass, c, charge_sensible_heat)\n\nMelt surface snow of column idx and convert it to liquid water, removing or merging depleted layers. melt_mass is the melt potential in kg m⁻² of ice at the melting point (the melt energy divided by Lm).\n\nWith charge_sensible_heat = true (melt computed from the surface energy balance), ice taken from a layer below T₀ first has to be warmed to T₀, so each kilogram costs Lm + ci (T₀ − T) and the melted mass is smaller than melt_mass. This keeps the column energy budget closed. With charge_sensible_heat = false (prescribed melt), exactly melt_mass is melted.\n\nReturns (melted, remaining): the melted snow mass and the melt potential left when the snow is exhausted (in kg m⁻² of ice at T₀).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._melt_surface_layers!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/accumulation_ablation/#Chion._apply_melt!-processes-accumulation_ablation","page":"Accumulation And Melt","text":"_apply_melt!(..., idx, mass_split, mass_min, melt_mass, c)\n\nConvert up to melt_mass of surface snow into liquid water in column idx (prescribed-melt semantics: no sensible-heat charge) and return the melted mass. See _melt_surface_layers!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._apply_melt!"},{"category":"section","location":"simulations/#Running-Simulations","page":"Running Simulations","text":"A Simulation wraps a model with its forcing years, a spin-up and its output. run! spins the model up, runs every forcing year and writes each output at its own frequency. The model holds its forcing source and reads it one year at a time, so long multi-file records fit in memory, also on a GPU node.\n\nusing Chion\nusing CUDA                        # only for the GPU\n\nforcing = Forcing(\"/data/MAR/MARv3.14.3-10km-daily-ERA5-{year}.nc\"; surface_fluxes=:parameterized)\nmodel = BESSIModel(forcing; backend=CUDABackend())    # CPU() (threads) by default\n\nsim = Simulation(model;\n    years=1940:2026,\n    spinup=CycledSpinup(1940:1969; cycles=5),\n    output=OutputConfig(dir=\"output/greenland\", restart_every=10,\n        outputs=[Output(\"smb_monthly.nc\"; variables=:monthly, frequency=:monthly),\n                 Output(\"budget_yearly.csv\"; variables=:budget, frequency=:yearly, aggregate=:area_sum)]))\nrun!(sim)                         # returns sim; the final state is sim.model.state\nsmbstats(sim)                     # column means (kg m⁻²) and totals (Gt) of the mass fluxes\n\nBESSIModel(forcing; NF, layering, params, backend, state) builds the Grid on the forcing columns; BESSIModel(grid; forcing, …) takes a grid built separately (see Models And Coupling). A GPU backend needs using CUDA first (configuration files run with run_config load it themselves); to_backend(model, CUDABackend()) moves an existing model. BESSIModel(...; state=...) starts from a given host state (e.g. from cpu_state) instead of the initial state of the parameters.","title":"Running Simulations"},{"category":"section","location":"simulations/#Run-directory-and-restarts","page":"Running Simulations","text":"output is an OutputConfig, or one Output or a list of them (written at their paths, without a run directory). With OutputConfig(; dir), the relative paths of the outputs are taken relative to the run directory dir, and the simulation also writes there\n\nlog.txt: one line per forcing year (column-mean SMB, melt, runoff; wall time),\nchion_run.toml: the grid, the parameters and the run settings in the format of run_config,\nrestart_<year>.jld2: the state every restart_every years and at the end of the run (restart_every = 0, the default: only at the end). A JLD2 (HDF5) file with the state arrays by name (state/mass, state/temperature, …, layers × columns) and the attributes format_version, model, number_type, columns, chion_version, last_year and next_year; it can also be read with h5py or h5dump.\n\nSimulation(model; years, restart=path) continues from a restart file and skips the spin-up; write_restart(sim, path) writes one at the start of any forcing year. sim.output.outputs lists the outputs of a simulation.","title":"Run directory and restarts"},{"category":"section","location":"simulations/#Stepping","page":"Running Simulations","text":"run! is resumable: run!(sim; years=1) runs the next forcing year only, and a further run!(sim) continues where it stopped. time_step!(sim) takes one forcing step, finished(sim) tells whether all years are done, and sim.clock holds the position (year index, step, iteration count, time). The outputs are closed when the last year is done.","title":"Stepping"},{"category":"section","location":"simulations/#Forcing","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Constructor Use\nForcing(\"dir/file_{year}.nc\"; …) one NetCDF file per year (missing years are skipped)\nForcing(\"file.nc\"; …) one file of any length; years are sliced from its time axis\nForcing(snowpack_forcing; grid, area) forcing already in memory\nForcing(year -> snowpack_forcing, years; grid, area) a function for preprocessing the loaders do not cover\n\nKeywords of the file constructors:\n\nformat: :mar for MAR output (variable names, ice mask MSK ≥ 50 %, a constant wind of 5 m s⁻¹, the cell area AREA), :alma for single-site forcing in the ALMA convention of land-surface intercomparisons such as ESM-SnowMIP (Tair, Qair, Psurf, Wind, Rainf, Snowf, SWdown, LWdown in SI units; gaps are filled with the last valid value and the specific humidity becomes relative humidity over ice), :generic for any other file, :auto to detect.\nsurface_fluxes (default :prescribed): :prescribed uses the surface energy fluxes of the file (MAR: net shortwave from SWD − SWU, LWD, SHF, LHF and the albedo AL2; use Params(albedo_scheme = PrescribedAlbedo()) in the model), :parameterized leaves them to Chion, :albedo keeps only the albedo (Chion computes the energy fluxes with it; use PrescribedAlbedo()), :as_loaded keeps whatever the loader provides. With the default :prescribed and a default BESSIModel (dynamic albedo), the MAR fluxes are combined with Chion's own albedo; pass surface_fluxes=:parameterized for a fully parameterised energy balance.\nvariables: names of a generic file, e.g. (air_temperature=\"t2m\", snowfall=\"sf\", rainfall=\"rf\", shortwave_down=\"ssrd\", latitude=\"lat\"); other load_forcing_file keywords (units, defaults) are passed through.\nfiles = Dict(year => path) replaces single years; mask = (name, threshold) selects columns; wind is a constant or a variable name; area the column areas (km²) as a vector or variable name, and area_file a separate file that holds it; years restricts the years used; transform = (forcing, year) -> forcing modifies every year (e.g. a temperature change).\ncache = dir keeps every decoded year in dir as a JLD2 file. A daily MAR year is a compressed NetCDF file of 2.3 GB; decompressing it and selecting the ice columns takes about 3 s, longer than a GPU needs to step the year. The first run writes the cache, later runs (and the later cycles of a spin-up) read a year in about 0.1 s, bitwise identical. A cache file is tied to its source file (path, size, modification time), the loader settings and the version of Chion's loaders, and is rewritten when one of them changes; transform is applied after the cache. Runs may share a directory (about 0.45 GB per Greenland year at 10 km).\n\nforcing_grid(forcing), forcing_years(forcing), forcing_area(forcing) and load_forcing_year(forcing, year) give access to the data, and slice_forcing(f, r) restricts a SnowpackForcing to the time indices r; Grid(forcing) and BESSIModel(forcing; kwargs...) build a grid and a model on the forcing columns. relabel_year(snowpack_forcing, year) moves the time stamps of one year's forcing to another year (the solar geometry stays), for running the weather of base years as future years, e.g. for a delta-method projection.","title":"Forcing"},{"category":"section","location":"simulations/#Spin-up","page":"Running Simulations","text":"CycledSpinup(years; cycles=5) runs the real forcing years in order, cycles times.\nClimatologySpinup(forcing; years=200) repeats another Forcing years times: the whole file of a single-file climatology (e.g. a day-by-day mean year), or the first year of a per-year source. In a configuration file, [spinup] with type = \"climatology\" and path takes the other settings (format, surface fluxes, …) from [forcing].\nNoSpinup() (the default) starts from the model's state: the initial state, or the state given to the model constructor (e.g. a restart).\n\nspin_up!(sim) runs the spin-up alone; run! and time_step! call it before the first step. years of the Simulation may repeat years, e.g. repeat(1940:1969, 5), to record every cycle of an equilibrium run.","title":"Spin-up"},{"category":"section","location":"simulations/#Outputs","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Output(path; variables=:default, frequency=:monthly, aggregate=:columns, kind=:mean,\n       period=nothing, regions=nothing, area=nothing, depths=0:0.5:20, snow_cover_threshold=5.0,\n       columns=nothing, grid=nothing)\n\npath: .nc (NetCDF, CF time axis with period bounds) or .csv (area aggregates or one column).\nfrequency: :step, :daily, :monthly, :yearly, or a period: Hour(3), Day(5), Week(1), Month(6), Year(10). Periods follow the calendar (months from January, days from 1970-01-01). Each record covers one period; its time is the start of the period and time_bnds gives the first and last step.\naggregate: :columns (one value per column, on the x–y grid when the grid has coordinates), :area_sum (fluxes and stores in Gt, snow cover in km², firn air content in km³, intensive quantities as area-weighted means) or :area_mean. regions takes a NamedTuple of column masks for several regions.\nkind: state variables as period means (:mean) or end-of-period values (:snapshot).\nperiod = (1980, 2017) writes only those years.\ncolumns records only a range of columns (e.g. one member of an ensemble run side by side), with grid their Grid.\n\nNetCDF files from a Simulation use a time dimension with time_bnds. The full model state at every forcing step is Output(path; variables=[:mass, :density, :temperature, …], frequency=:step, kind=:snapshot). available_output_variables(model) lists the variables a model can write. The table below is for BESSIModel; PDDModel and ITMModel write the forcing fluxes and their own state and budget fields (snowpack_swe, smb_ice, runoff, … and H_snow, melt, refreezing, …).\n\nVariable Kind Units \nsurface_smb flux kg m⁻² precipitation − runoff + vapour exchange\nsmb_ice flux kg m⁻² net mass forcing to the ice sheet\nmelt, runoff, refreezing, sublimation flux kg m⁻² \nvapor_mass flux kg m⁻² net vapour exchange (deposition − sublimation − evaporation)\nprecipitation, snowfall, rainfall flux kg m⁻² from the forcing\nlatent_heat_flux, sensible_heat_flux rate W m⁻² period mean\nshortwave_down, shortwave_net, longwave_down, longwave_net, rain_heat_flux rate W m⁻² period mean; net = absorbed, positive into the surface\nswe, snow_mass, liquid_water state kg m⁻² solid + liquid, solid, liquid\nthickness, bulk_density, firn_air_content state m, kg m⁻³, m \nsnow_cover state 1 SWE ≥ snow_cover_threshold; its mean is the snow-cover frequency\nalbedo, Tsrf, snow_age_days, mass_base, N state  \nmass, mass_w, density, temperature snapshot  per snow layer\nice_temperature snapshot K per ice-substrate layer\ndensity_profile, temperature_profile snapshot kg m⁻³, K on the depth bins / at the depths depths\n\nGroups: :budget (SMB, precipitation, melt, runoff, refreezing, vapour exchange), :monthly (the monthly mass-budget and surface energy balance maps), :snow, :firn and :default.\n\nFlux totals of a fine frequency add up to those of a coarser one to rounding precision; the tests check daily against monthly and yearly totals.","title":"Outputs"},{"category":"section","location":"simulations/#Configuration-files","page":"Running Simulations","text":"The same simulation as TOML, run with julia --project -m Chion file.toml (Julia ≥ 1.11) or run_config(\"file.toml\"). --backend=gpu and --output-dir=DIR override the file, and --set section.key=value overrides any entry (e.g. --set model.alpha_dry=0.84; in Julia run_config(path; set=[\"model.alpha_dry\" => 0.84])). run_config(path; dry_run=true) returns the Simulation without running it. output_dir is the run directory of the OutputConfig: the outputs, the log, the restarts and a copy of the file (with the overrides applied) are written there.\n\nname = \"greenland_parameterized\"\nbackend = \"gpu\"\nyears = [1940, 2026]          # first and last year; `repeat = 5` runs them five times\noutput_dir = \"output/greenland\"   # the run directory\nrestart_every = 10            # a restart file every 10 years (0: only at the end)\n\n[forcing]\npath = \"/data/MAR/MARv3.14.3-10km-daily-ERA5-{year}.nc\"\nformat = \"mar\"\nsurface_fluxes = \"parameterized\"\n[forcing.files]\n2026 = \"/data/MAR/MARv3.14.3-10km-daily-ERA5-2026_repaired.nc\"\n\n[grid]                        # Layering fields; omitted ones keep their defaults\nNtot = 15\n\n[model]                       # model type, named setup, number type and Params fields\ntype = \"bessi\"\nsetup = \"GrISv1\"              # or \"AISv1\", the Antarctic calibration\nnumber_type = \"Float64\"\nalbedo_scheme = \"dynamic\"     # a scheme by name, with its default coefficients\n\n[model.diurnal_cycle]         # or a scheme as a table: its name under `type`, and coefficients\ntype = \"substeps\"\nmax_substeps = 8\n\n[spinup]\ntype = \"cycled\"               # none, cycled or climatology\nyears = [1940, 1969]\ncycles = 5\n\n[[output]]\nfile = \"monthly.nc\"\nvariables = \"monthly\"\nfrequency = \"monthly\"         # step, daily, monthly, yearly, \"5 days\", \"6 months\", …\n\n[[output]]\nfile = \"budget_yearly.csv\"\nvariables = \"budget\"\nfrequency = \"yearly\"\naggregate = \"area_sum\"\n\nFurther keys:\n\nSection Keys\ntop level name, backend (threads or gpu), output_dir, restart_every, years ([first, last], \"1940:2026\" or one year), repeat\n[forcing] path, format, surface_fluxes, files, mask = [\"MSK\", 50], wind, area, cache (a directory, relative to the file), area_file, variables = {air_temperature = \"t2m\", …}, options = {…} (other Forcing keywords)\n[grid] any Layering field, e.g. Ntot = 20, near_surface_layer_max_thicknesses_m = [0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.3], ice_substrate_layers = 0\n[model] type = \"bessi\", \"pdd\" or \"itm\"; setup = \"GrISv1\" or \"AISv1\" (BESSI only); number_type = \"Float32\"; any field of Params, PDDParams or ITMParams, e.g. alpha_dry = 0.84, albedo_scheme = \"aging\", [model.albedo_scheme] type = \"aging\", cold_timescale_days = 15, method = \"pism\"\n[spinup] type, years, cycles; for climatology a path and an optional [spinup.forcing] table that overrides [forcing]\n[[output]] file, variables, frequency, aggregate, kind, period = [1980, 2017], depths = {start = 0, stop = 20, step = 0.5}, snow_cover_threshold\n\nThe scheme names are listed on Parameters. A --set override reaches the coefficients of a scheme table, e.g. --set model.diurnal_cycle.max_substeps=4.","title":"Configuration files"},{"category":"section","location":"simulations/#Published-Setups","page":"Running Simulations","text":"SimulationGreenlandMARv1(path) and SimulationAntarcticaMARv1(path, climatology) build the Greenland and Antarctic runs of the Chion paper in one call (as Laddie's reproductions): forcing, grid, calibration, spin-up, years and, with dir, the outputs. test/reproductions.jl pins their totals for one MAR year.\n\nsim = SimulationGreenlandMARv1(\"/data/MAR/MARv3.14.3-10km-daily-ERA5-{year}.nc\"; dir = \"output/gris\",\n    files = Dict(2026 => \"/data/MAR/MARv3.14.3-10km-daily-ERA5-2026_repaired.nc\"))\nrun!(sim)","title":"Published Setups"},{"category":"section","location":"simulations/#Debugging","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Simulation(model; debug = DebugConfig(check_nans = true)) checks the state of the active columns for NaN and Inf after every launch of the column step and stops with the variable, column and date.","title":"Debugging"},{"category":"section","location":"simulations/#Reference","page":"Running Simulations","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Simulation","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct Simulation{M<:Chion.AbstractSnowModel, SP<:Chion.AbstractSpinup}\n\nA model with its forcing years, spin-up and outputs. Build with Simulation(model; …) and advance with run! or time_step!.\n\nFields\n\nmodel::Chion.AbstractSnowModel: the model being integrated\nclock::Clock: position in the forcing years\nyears::Vector{Int64}: forcing years, in the order they are run (they may repeat)\nspinup::Chion.AbstractSpinup: spin-up before the first year\noutput::OutputConfig: run directory, field outputs, log, metadata and restarts\nio::Chion.IOState: run-time state of the outputs\nname::String: name of the run (in logs and output metadata)\nverbose::Bool: whether run! prints a line per year\ndebug::DebugConfig: debug options\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.SimulationGreenlandMARv1","page":"Running Simulations","text":"SimulationGreenlandMARv1(path; years = 1940:2026, spinup = CycledSpinup(1940:1969; cycles = 5),\n    surface_fluxes = :parameterized, files = Dict{Int, String}(), NF = Float64, backend = CPU(),\n    params = ParamsGrISv1(; NF), dir = nothing, forcing_cache = nothing, name = \"greenland_mar_v1\",\n    verbose = true)\n\nThe Greenland run of the Chion paper: BESSIModel with the Greenland calibration on the ice columns (MAR mask ≥ 50 %) of daily MAR v3.14.3 (10 km, ERA5), five spin-up cycles of 1940–1969, then 1940–2026. path is the pattern of the yearly MAR files with {year}; files replaces single years (the paper used a repaired 2026 file). surface_fluxes = :prescribed (with params = ParamsGrISv1(; NF, albedo_scheme = PrescribedAlbedo())) gives the prescribed-flux run. With a run directory dir, writes monthly.nc and budget_yearly.csv there; forcing_cache is a directory for the decoded forcing years (the cache of Forcing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.SimulationGreenlandMARv1"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.SimulationAntarcticaMARv1","page":"Running Simulations","text":"SimulationAntarcticaMARv1(path, climatology; years = 1979:2026, spinup_years = 200,\n    surface_fluxes = :parameterized, area_file = nothing, NF = Float64, backend = CPU(),\n    params = ParamsGrISv1(; NF), dir = nothing, forcing_cache = nothing, name = \"antarctica_mar_v1\",\n    verbose = true)\n\nThe Antarctic run of the Chion paper: BESSIModel on the ice columns of daily MAR v3.14.3 (27.5 km, ERA5), spun up with spinup_years repetitions of the single-year file climatology (the paper: 200 years of 1979), then 1979–2026. The paper used the Greenland calibration; params = ParamsAISv1(; NF) gives the Antarctic one. area_file is a MAR file with the cell areas. With a run directory dir, writes monthly.nc and budget_yearly.csv there; forcing_cache is a directory for the decoded forcing years (the cache of Forcing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.SimulationAntarcticaMARv1"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.DebugConfig","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct DebugConfig\n\nDebug options of a Simulation.\n\nSet check_nans = true to check the prognostic state of the active columns for NaN and Inf after every launch of the column step (one step when outputs accumulate every step, else up to the next output period). The run then errors at once, naming the first variable to blow up, the column and the date. Costs a host copy of the state per launch.\n\nFields\n\ncheck_nans::Bool = false: check the state for NaN and Inf after every launch of the column step (default false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.DebugConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Clock","page":"Running Simulations","text":"mutable struct Clock\n\nPosition of a Simulation in its forcing years. Never reset by run!, so successive calls continue where the previous one stopped.\n\nFields\n\nyear_index::Int64: index in sim.years of the current year (length(sim.years) + 1 when finished)\nstep::Int64: next forcing step of the current year\niteration::Int64: forcing steps taken (spin-up excluded)\ntime::Dates.DateTime: start of the next forcing step (the end of the last one when finished)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Clock"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.run!","page":"Running Simulations","text":"run!(sim; years = nothing)\n\nSpin up (on the first call), then run the remaining forcing years of sim, or the next years of them, writing the outputs. Successive calls continue where the previous one stopped; the outputs are closed when the last year is done. Returns sim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.run!"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.time_step!","page":"Running Simulations","text":"time_step!(sim)\n\nTake one forcing step of sim (after the spin-up, on the first call), recording the outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.time_step!"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.spin_up!","page":"Running Simulations","text":"spin_up!(sim)\n\nRun the spin-up of sim (once; run! and time_step! call it before the first step).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.spin_up!"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.finished","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Whether all forcing years of sim have been run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.finished"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.smbstats","page":"Running Simulations","text":"smbstats(sim_or_model) -> NamedTuple\n\nColumn means (kg m⁻²) of the cumulative mass fluxes since the start of the model, and with column areas in the forcing their totals (Gt): smb_ice, melt, runoff, refreezing (BESSI also sublimation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.smbstats"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.NoSpinup","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct NoSpinup <: Chion.AbstractSpinup\n\nNo spin-up: start from the model's state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.NoSpinup"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.CycledSpinup","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct CycledSpinup <: Chion.AbstractSpinup\n\nRun the forcing years years in order, cycles times, without output.\n\nCycledSpinup(years; cycles = 5)\n\nFields\n\nyears::Vector{Int64}: forcing years of one cycle\ncycles::Int64: number of cycles\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.CycledSpinup"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.ClimatologySpinup","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct ClimatologySpinup{F<:Forcing} <: Chion.AbstractSpinup\n\nRun the years of another forcing (e.g. a day-by-day climatology) years times, without output.\n\nClimatologySpinup(forcing::Forcing; years = 200)\n\nFields\n\nforcing::Forcing: the forcing of the spin-up\nyears::Int64: number of repetitions of its years\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ClimatologySpinup"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.OutputConfig","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct OutputConfig\n\nWhere and what a Simulation writes.\n\nOutputConfig(; dir = nothing, outputs = Output[], log = true, metadata = true, restart_every = 0)\n\nWith a run directory dir, the relative paths of the Outputs are taken relative to it, and the simulation writes there:\n\nlog.txt: one line per forcing year (column-mean SMB, melt, runoff; wall time),\nchion_run.toml: the grid, the parameters and the run settings in the format of run_config (rerunning it with the forcing section added reproduces the run),\nrestart_<year>.jld2 every restart_every years and at the end of the run (0: only at the end), read by Simulation(model; restart = path).\n\nWithout dir only the outputs are written, at their paths.\n\nFields\n\ndir::Union{Nothing, String}: run directory, or nothing\noutputs::Vector{Output}: field outputs, one file each, with paths resolved against dir\nlog::Bool: whether to write log.txt\nmetadata::Bool: whether to write chion_run.toml\nrestart_every::Int64: years between restart files (0: only at the end); restarts need dir\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.OutputConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Output","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Output(path; variables=:default, frequency=:monthly, aggregate=:columns, kind=:mean,\n       period=nothing, regions=nothing, area=nothing, depths=0:0.5:20, snow_cover_threshold=5.0,\n       columns=nothing, grid=nothing)\n\npath: .nc (NetCDF) or .csv (only for area-aggregated output or a single column).\nvariables: a list of names or a group (:budget, :monthly, :default, :snow, :firn); see available_output_variables(model).\nfrequency: :step, :daily, :monthly, :yearly, or a period such as Day(5), Month(6) (or the types EveryStep(), Daily(), Monthly(), Yearly(), Every(Month(6))). Periods are aligned to the calendar (months from January, days from 1970-01-01).\naggregate: :columns (one value per column, on the x–y grid when the grid has coordinates), :area_sum (mass fluxes and stores in Gt, snow cover in km², firn air content in km³, intensive variables as area-weighted means) or :area_mean (all area-weighted means).\nkind: :mean (period mean) or :snapshot (end of period) for state variables (or Mean(), Snapshot()); aggregate also takes Columns(), AreaSum(), AreaMean().\nperiod: (first, last) years or dates; records outside are not written.\nregions: for area aggregation, a NamedTuple of masks (column vectors or y–x matrices), e.g. (eurasia=mask1, north_america=mask2); default all columns.\narea: column areas in km² (default: from the forcing, e.g. MAR AREA).\ndepths: bin edges (m) of :density_profile and depths of :temperature_profile.\ncolumns: record only these columns of the domain (a unit range), e.g. one member of an ensemble run side by side; grid is their Grid (default: no spatial coordinates), and regions and area refer to them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Output"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.EveryStep","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct EveryStep <: Chion.AbstractOutputFrequency\n\nOne record per forcing step (frequency = :step).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.EveryStep"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Daily","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct Daily <: Chion.AbstractOutputFrequency\n\nOne record per calendar day (frequency = :daily).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Daily"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Monthly","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct Monthly <: Chion.AbstractOutputFrequency\n\nOne record per calendar month (frequency = :monthly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Monthly"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Yearly","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct Yearly <: Chion.AbstractOutputFrequency\n\nOne record per calendar year (frequency = :yearly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Yearly"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Every","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct Every{P<:Dates.Period} <: Chion.AbstractOutputFrequency\n\nOne record per period (a Dates.Period such as Month(6)), aligned to the calendar: months from January, days from 1970-01-01.\n\nFields\n\nperiod::Dates.Period: length of the period\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Every"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Columns","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct Columns <: Chion.AbstractAggregation\n\nOne value per column, on the x–y grid when the grid has coordinates (aggregate = :columns).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Columns"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.AreaSum","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct AreaSum <: Chion.AbstractAggregation\n\nArea sums: mass in Gt, snow cover in km², firn air content in km³, intensive variables as area-weighted means (aggregate = :area_sum).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AreaSum"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.AreaMean","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct AreaMean <: Chion.AbstractAggregation\n\nArea-weighted means of every variable (aggregate = :area_mean).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AreaMean"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Mean","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct Mean <: Chion.AbstractOutputKind\n\nPeriod means of the state variables (kind = :mean).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Mean"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Snapshot","page":"Running Simulations","text":"struct Snapshot <: Chion.AbstractOutputKind\n\nState variables at the end of the period (kind = :snapshot).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Snapshot"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.write_restart","page":"Running Simulations","text":"write_restart(sim, path)\n\nWrite the host state of sim's model and the next forcing year to path, a JLD2 (HDF5) file read by Simulation(model; restart = path). The state arrays are stored by name under state/ (e.g. state/mass, layers × columns), so the file can also be read outside Julia (h5py, h5dump); the attributes format_version, model, number_type, columns, chion_version, last_year and next_year (absent at the end of the run) describe it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.write_restart"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.available_output_variables","page":"Running Simulations","text":"All output variables of model, for documentation and error messages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.available_output_variables"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.run_config","page":"Running Simulations","text":"run_config(path; backend = nothing, output_dir = nothing, set = (), dry_run = false) -> Simulation\n\nBuild the Simulation described by the TOML file path and run it. backend (:threads or :gpu) and output_dir override the values in the file; set overrides any entry, e.g. set = [\"model.alpha_dry\" => 0.84, \"model.diurnal_cycle.max_substeps\" => 4]. dry_run = true returns the simulation without running it.\n\nThe file has the run settings (name, backend, years, repeat, output_dir, the run directory, and restart_every) and the tables [forcing], [grid] (the Layering), [boundary] (surface, bottom), [model] (type, setup, number_type and any field of the parameters; a scheme as a name or a table with type), [spinup] and [[output]]; see examples/configs/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.run_config"},{"category":"type","location":"simulations/#Chion.Forcing","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Forcing(path; format=:auto, surface_fluxes=:prescribed, years=nothing, files=Dict(),\n        mask=nothing, wind=nothing, area=:auto, transform=nothing, variables=nothing, kwargs...)\n\npath: a file, or a pattern with {year} for one file per year (missing years are skipped).\nformat: :mar (MAR daily output: variable names, the ice mask MSK ≥ 50 %, a constant wind of 5 m s⁻¹ and the cell area AREA), :alma (single-site forcing in the ALMA convention of land-surface model intercomparisons such as ESM-SnowMIP: Tair, Qair, Psurf, Wind, Rainf, Snowf, SWdown, LWdown in SI units) or :generic (names from variables and kwargs, as in load_forcing_file). :auto picks :mar when the file has MSK and TTZ/TT, and :alma when it has Tair and Qair.\nsurface_fluxes: :prescribed uses the surface energy fluxes of the file (for MAR the net shortwave from SWD − SWU, LWD, SHF, LHF and the albedo AL2), :parameterized drops them so that Chion computes them, :albedo keeps only the albedo (Chion computes the energy fluxes with it; use with albedo=:prescribed in the model), :as_loaded keeps whatever the loader provides.\nfiles: Dict(year => path) overrides single years (e.g. a repaired last year).\nmask: (name, threshold) selects columns, e.g. (\"MSK\", 50.0).\nwind: constant wind speed (m s⁻¹) or the name of a wind variable.\narea: column areas (km²): :auto (MAR AREA), a variable name, a vector, or nothing.\ntransform: (forcing, year) -> forcing, applied to every year (e.g. a temperature change).\nvariables: NamedTuple of variable names for :generic files, e.g. (air_temperature=\"t2m\", snowfall=\"sf\", rainfall=\"rf\", shortwave_down=\"ssrd\").\ncache: a directory for decoded forcing years, or nothing. Reading a compressed MAR year (decompression, column selection, unit conversion) takes seconds; with a cache the first run writes every year it loads as a JLD2 file there, and later runs read it back in a fraction of a second, bitwise identical. A cache file belongs to one forcing file (path, size, modification time), the loader settings (format, surface_fluxes, mask, wind, variables, kwargs) and the version of Chion's loaders; when one changes, the year is loaded again and the file replaced. transform is applied after the cache. Runs may share a cache directory.\n\n\n\n\n\nForcing(forcing::SnowpackForcing; grid=Grid(ncol), area=nothing, transform=nothing)\n\nWrap forcing that is already in memory. Years are sliced from its time axis.\n\n\n\n\n\nForcing(f, years; grid, area=nothing, transform=nothing)\n\nForcing from a user function f(year) -> SnowpackForcing (for preprocessing that the file loaders do not cover). grid is the Grid of the columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Forcing"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.forcing_grid","page":"Running Simulations","text":"The Grid of the forcing columns (default layering, Float64).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.forcing_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.forcing_years","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Years the forcing can provide.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.forcing_years"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.forcing_area","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Column areas in km², or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.forcing_area"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.load_forcing_year","page":"Running Simulations","text":"Forcing of one year, checked against the grid of the first year and transformed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.load_forcing_year"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.slice_forcing","page":"Running Simulations","text":"The forcing restricted to the time indices r.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.slice_forcing"},{"category":"function","location":"simulations/#Chion.relabel_year","page":"Running Simulations","text":"relabel_year(forcing, year)\n\nforcing with its time stamps moved to year (29 February becomes 28 February), for running the weather of one year as another, e.g. base years under a projected warming. The day of year and the solar geometry stay those of the original year.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.relabel_year"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/test_energy_flux_analytical/#Energy-Flux-Analytical-Validation-Note","page":"Energy Flux Analytical Tests","text":"This page summarizes the analytical checks of the energy solve with the Robin surface boundary (see Energy Balance). They are executed in test/test_longwave_consistency.jl and test/test_case_api.jl.","title":"Energy Flux Analytical Validation Note"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/test_energy_flux_analytical/#Insulated-Equilibrium","page":"Energy Flux Analytical Tests","text":"With all surface fluxes and emission switched off and a uniform temperature, the solve must leave interface and layer temperatures unchanged and diagnose no melt. This is checked for a regular (100 kg m⁻²) and a vanishingly thin (10⁻⁸ kg m⁻²) surface layer: eliminating the interface temperature must not create a numerically singular top row, so the diagonal stays exactly one.","title":"Insulated Equilibrium"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/test_energy_flux_analytical/#Graybody-Radiative-Equilibrium","page":"Energy Flux Analytical Tests","text":"A surface at T0 under incident longwave sigma T_0^4 from an isothermal blackbody environment absorbs and emits the same graybody flux regardless of emissivity, so neither snow nor bare ice may warm or melt. The original BESSI treatment (BESSISurfaceEnergyBalance(), unit incident absorption) is checked to retain its spurious net gain (1-epsilon)sigma T_0^4.","title":"Graybody Radiative Equilibrium"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/test_energy_flux_analytical/#Interface-Conductance","page":"Energy Flux Analytical Tests","text":"For two layers under pure diffusion, the heat exchanged between them must follow the harmonic interface conductance G_i+12 = 2K_iK_i+1(K_i+1Delta z_i + K_iDelta z_i+1).","title":"Interface Conductance"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/test_energy_flux_analytical/#Melting-Boundary","page":"Energy Flux Analytical Tests","text":"When the surface energy balance would raise the interface above T0:\n\nmelt is diagnosed even if the centre of the top cell is still below T0;\nthe conduction G_s(T_0 - T_1) from the melting surface into the snow below is retained, and the melt energy is the remainder of the surface energy balance;\nthe sensible-energy change of the column plus the melt energy equals the energy supplied at the surface.","title":"Melting Boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/test_energy_flux_analytical/#Ice-Substrate","page":"Energy Flux Analytical Tests","text":"On a bare-ice column the public go_energy_flux! solves the ice substrate: a negative surface energy balance cools both the interface and the top substrate layer below T0 instead of being discarded.","title":"Ice Substrate"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Energy-Balance","page":"Energy Balance","text":"go_energy_flux! solves the temperature profile of the snow/firn column and the optional ice substrate below it with one implicit conductive step and a linearized surface energy balance. The surface temperature Tsrf is the temperature of the snow–air interface; it carries no heat capacity of its own (Robin boundary). The formulas below follow src/processes/energy_flux.jl and src/processes/surface_fluxes.jl.\n\nFor daily forcing, the default Diurnal Shortwave Cycle subdivides a forcing step before this energy solve is applied.","title":"Energy Balance"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Surface-Flux-Parameterization","page":"Energy Balance","text":"At the surface, the model combines absorbed shortwave radiation, longwave radiation, sensible heat exchange, and precipitation / latent-heat terms into the surface energy balance Q(T_s) = Q_sw + Q_lw + Q_sh + Q_p + Q_lh. The terms are linearized about the previous surface temperature,\n\nQ(T_s) approx Q_mathrmconst - Q_mathrmlin T_s\n\nand stored as surface_flux_constant and surface_flux_linear.","title":"Surface Flux Parameterization"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Robin-Surface-Boundary","page":"Energy Balance","text":"The interface temperature balances the surface energy balance against the conductive flux into the first layer,\n\nQ(T_s) = G_s(T_s - T_1)\nqquad\nG_s = frac2K_1Delta z_1\n\nwhere T_1, K_1 and Delta z_1 are the temperature, conductivity and thickness of the first layer (its centre lies Delta z_12 below the surface). Solving for the interface temperature,\n\nT_s = fracQ_mathrmconst + G_s T_1Q_mathrmlin + G_s\n\nand eliminating it leaves the first layer as a regular finite-volume cell,\n\nc_i m_1 fracpartial T_1partial t\n= G_s(T_s - T_1) + G_32(T_2 - T_1)\n\nso the surface forcing enters only the top row of the implicit tridiagonal system. A thin top layer therefore responds quickly without an artificial surface heat capacity.\n\nParams uses turbulent_flux_scheme = SemixTurbulentFluxes() by default for sensible and latent heat. Set turbulent_flux_scheme = BESSITurbulentFluxes() to recover the BESSI bulk formulation. This choice is independent of surface_energy_balance_scheme, which defaults to SemixSurfaceEnergyBalance() for consistent graybody longwave exchange, including incident absorption. Set surface_energy_balance_scheme = BESSISurfaceEnergyBalance() for the legacy longwave treatment. Prescribed q_sh or q_lh forcing always takes precedence over either parameterization.","title":"Robin Surface Boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Shortwave-Radiation","page":"Energy Balance","text":"If q_sw_net is not prescribed, absorbed shortwave is\n\nQ_sw = (1-alpha)Q_mathrmswdown\n\nwhere alpha is the already diagnosed surface albedo stored in albedo[idx].","title":"Shortwave Radiation"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Longwave-Radiation","page":"Energy Balance","text":"If q_lw_down is not prescribed, the default longwave_scheme = CloudProxyLongwave() derives it once per daily forcing step as q_mathrmlwdown = epsilon_asigma T_mathrmair^4. The effective emissivity weights a clear-sky and an overcast emissivity by a cloud fraction n (Konzelmann et al., 1994),\n\nepsilon_a = epsilon_cs(T_mathrmair)(1 - n^q) + epsilon_oc(T_mathrmair)n^q\nqquad\nepsilon_csoc = epsilon_csoc0 + epsilon_csocT(T_mathrmair - T_0)\n\nand is limited to 04 le epsilon_a le 1 (emissivity_max). The cloud fraction is diagnosed from the daily shortwave transmissivity tau = Q_mathrmswdownQ_mathrmswTOA by inverting the cloud transmission tautau_cs = 1 - an^p e^-b z, with the clear-sky transmissivity tau_cs = tau_0 + tau_z z + tau_S ln(Q_mathrmswTOA300mathrmWm^-2) (at most one; clear_sky_transmissivity(params, z, Q_sw_TOA)). All coefficients were fitted to daily MAR over the Greenland ice sheet (1980–1989) with examples/research/fit_cloud_proxy_longwave.jl: tau_0 = 0718, tau_z = 00298mathrmkm^-1, tau_S = 00569, a = 0447, p = 275, b = 0225mathrmkm^-1, epsilon_cs0 = 0716, epsilon_csT = 000239mathrmK^-1, epsilon_oc0 = 0952, epsilon_ocT = 000362mathrmK^-1, and q = 3. The daily-mean top-of-atmosphere insolation Q_mathrmswTOA is computed from latitude and solar declination with a solar constant of 1361 W m⁻² and the eccentricity factor 1 + 0033cos(2pi d365) for day of year d. In the polar night (Q_mathrmswTOA le 50 W m⁻²), for sub-daily forcing whose shortwave is not a daily mean (dt_days < 0.75), and for forcing without latitude, n is the constant night_cloud_fraction (0.85). Without a surface height the sea-level coefficients are used. No forcing beyond the standard shortwave is needed.\n\n(Image: Fit of the longwave parameterisation to daily MAR over Greenland)\n\nFit of the cloud-proxy longwave to daily MAR over the Greenland ice sheet. (a) Clear-sky shortwave transmissivity against elevation (MAR median and 10–90 % range, 1980–1989) and the fit for daily-mean insolation of 150, 300 and 450 W m⁻². (b) Cloud transmission tautau_cs against MAR cloud cover for three elevation bands. (c) Diagnosed cloud fraction against MAR cloud cover, 2010–2019 (r = 0.78). (d) Effective emissivity of MAR against the diagnosed cloud fraction for four air-temperature classes and the fit. (e) Daily downwelling longwave of Chion against MAR, 2010–2019 (bias −0.2 W m⁻², RMSE 15.9 W m⁻²). (f) Bias against elevation for summer, summer melt days and the annual mean.\n\nThe longwave is computed once per forcing interval and held fixed across the diurnal substeps. The coefficients above are fields of CloudProxyLongwave. The defaults are fitted for Greenland. The Antarctic set used for the paper runs (clear_sky_emissivity=0.7314, clear_sky_emissivity_temperature_slope=0.003378, overcast_emissivity=0.9877, overcast_emissivity_temperature_slope=0.004171, cloud_emissivity_exponent=2.0, night_cloud_fraction=0.9) is part of the named setup ParamsAISv1 (setup = \"AISv1\" in a configuration file).\n\nlongwave_scheme = GraybodyLongwave() uses the constant epsilon_mathrmair (ϵ_air, 0.8) instead:\n\nQ_mathrmlw =\nepsilon_mathrmsnowsigmaleft(epsilon_mathrmair T_mathrmair^4\n- T_s^4right)\n\nThe emitted longwave term is nonlinear in surface temperature, so the solver linearizes it about the previous-step temperature T_s^n:\n\n(T_s^n+1)^4 approx 4(T_s^n)^3T_s^n+1-3(T_s^n)^4\n\nThis gives the implemented constant and linear parts\n\nQ_mathrmlwconst =\nepsilon_mathrmsleft(\nq_mathrmlwdown\n+ 3sigma(T_s^n)^4\nright)\nqquad\nQ_mathrmlwlin =\n4sigmaepsilon_mathrms(T_s^n)^3\n\nwhere q_mathrmlwdown is the prescribed, cloud-proxy or graybody incident longwave and epsilon_mathrms is the surface emissivity: ϵ_snow (0.98) over snow and eps_ice (0.98) over bare ice. Only outgoing longwave is linearized. The energy diagnostics record the nonlinear flux at the resolved surface temperature. BESSISurfaceEnergyBalance() (legacy BESSI) absorbs the incident longwave completely (no epsilon_s factor on q_mathrmlwdown) and uses ϵ_snow for emission over snow and bare ice alike.","title":"Longwave Radiation"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Sensible-Heat","page":"Energy Balance","text":"If q_sh is not prescribed, the default SEMIX scheme follows the bulk-aerodynamic formulation of PALADYN (Willeit and Ganopolski, 2016) as used in SEMIX (Willeit et al., 2024). The neutral transfer coefficient at the reference height z = 10 m (reference_height) is\n\nC_H^0 = frackappa^2ln(zz_0m)ln(zz_0h)\n\nwith kappa = 04 (karman), the momentum roughness z0m_snow (10^-3 m) or z0m_ice (10^-2 m) over bare ice, and z_0h = z_0m zm_to_zh (10), all fields of SemixTurbulentFluxes. Stability enters through the bulk Richardson number and the aerodynamic resistance,\n\nRi_B = fracg z (T_mathrmair-T_s)T_mathrmairtilde u^2\nqquad\nr_a = left(C_H^0 f_mathrmstabtilde uright)^-1\nqquad\nf_mathrmstab =\nbegincases\nsqrt1-16Ri_B  Ri_B0\n(1+bRi_B)^-1  Ri_Bgeq0\nendcases\n\nwith tilde u = max(u 01mathrmms^-1) and b = stable_coefficient (40). Turbulent exchange is therefore suppressed under the stable conditions that prevail over melting and cold ice-sheet surfaces. The sensible heat flux is\n\nQ_mathrmsh = f_Hfracrho_mathrmairc_pmathrmairr_a\n(T_mathrmair-T_s)\nqquad\nrho_mathrmair = fracpR_d T_mathrmair\n\nwith R_d = 28705 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ and the exchange factor f_H = sensible_exchange_factor (3.0, calibrated against MAR over Greenland; 2.5 for the Antarctic set). r_a is evaluated at the surface temperature of the previous step and held fixed in the implicit solve. Without wind or pressure forcing, u = 5 m s⁻¹ and p follows from the surface height with the barometric formula (or 101 325 Pa).\n\nWith turbulent_flux_scheme = BESSITurbulentFluxes(), the code instead uses\n\nQ_mathrmsh= D_mathrmsh(T_mathrmair-T_s)\n\nwith default D_mathrmsh = 10mathrmWm^-2K^-1 (D_sh). In the linearized form,\n\nQ_mathrmshconst = D_mathrmshT_mathrmair\nqquad\nQ_mathrmshlin = D_mathrmsh","title":"Sensible Heat"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Precipitation-And-Latent-Heat-Terms","page":"Energy Balance","text":"Snowfall and rain heat terms are directly diagnosed from the forcing rates in kg m^-2 s^-1.\n\nFor snowfall, \n\nH_mathrmsnow = P_mathrmsnow c_i\nqquad\nK_mathrmsnow = P_mathrmsnow c_i T_mathrmair\n\nso the snowfall contribution can be written as\n\nQ_mathrmsnow(T_s) =\nK_mathrmsnow - H_mathrmsnowT_s\n=\nP_mathrmsnow c_i (T_mathrmair - T_s)\n\nFor rainfall onto snow (or, with the ice substrate, onto bare ice), the current implementation uses a temperature-independent term: rain enters at the melting point and releases the heat it carries above T_0,\n\nQ_mathrmrain =\nP_mathrmrain c_w (T_mathrmair - T_0)\n\nIf q_lh is prescribed, the code treats it as an additional turbulent latent heat flux. It is added to these snowfall/rain heat-term diagnoses rather than replacing them. If q_lh is not prescribed and relative humidity is available, the default SEMIX parameterization diagnoses humidity exchange using the same stability-corrected aerodynamic resistance as sensible heat:\n\nQ_mathrmvap =\nf_EfracL(T_s)rho_mathrmairr_a(q_mathrmair-q_s)\nqquad\nq_mathrmair = 0622RHfrace_mathrmsati(T_mathrmair)p\nqquad\nq_s = 0622frace_mathrmsati(T_s)p\n\nwith f_E = latent_exchange_factor (1.0) and the saturation vapour pressure over ice e_mathrmsati(T) = 6112mathrmPaexpleft(2246fracT-T_0T-T_0+27262mathrmKright). The latent heat follows the surface phase: L = L_v + L_m (sublimation) for a subfreezing surface and L = L_v (evaporation) at the melting point. With BESSITurbulentFluxes(), Chion instead uses the vapor-pressure parameterization of Rolstad and Oerlemans (2005) and Zolles and Born (2021)\n\nQ_mathrmvap =\nfracD_mathrmlfp_mathrmair\nleft(RHe_mathrmsatw(T_mathrmair) - e_mathrmsati(T_s)right)\n\nwith\n\nD_mathrmlf =\nrfracD_mathrmshc_pmathrmair0622L(T_s)\n\nwhere the air vapour pressure is computed over water, e_mathrmsatw(T) = 6112mathrmPaexpleft(1727fracT-T_0T-T_0+24312mathrmKright). Here r is latent_heat_flux_ratio, defaulting to 1.0. In both schemes the saturation vapor pressure over ice is linearized about the previous surface temperature for the implicit surface solve, and L is evaluated at that temperature. Bare ice without a substrate always uses L_v + L_m.\n\nThe latent heat flux is accompanied by a mass exchange with the atmosphere. In the SEMIX scheme and for prescribed q_lh, the vapour mass flux is Q_mathrmvapL(T_s). The BESSI scheme computes it directly from the vapour-pressure gradient. After the energy solve, a subfreezing surface exchanges solid mass of the uppermost layer (sublimation and deposition), and a surface at the melting point exchanges liquid water (evaporation and condensation). Sublimation is limited to the mass available in the surface layer. relative_humidity may be supplied either as a fraction (0.0 to 1.0) or as percent (0.0 to 100.0). If neither q_lh nor relative humidity is available, the turbulent latent heat term is zero.\n\nWhen both snowfall and rainfall are positive, the snowfall branch takes precedence in _diagnose_latent_heat_flux_coefficients.","title":"Precipitation And Latent-Heat Terms"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Combined-Linearized-Surface-Forcing","page":"Energy Balance","text":"Collecting the terms used by the implementation gives\n\nQ_mathrmconst =\nQ_sw\n+ Q_mathrmlwconst\n+ Q_mathrmshconst\n+ K_mathrmsnowrainlatent\nqquad\nQ_mathrmlin =\nQ_mathrmlwlin\n+ Q_mathrmshlin\n+ H_mathrmsnowlatent","title":"Combined Linearized Surface Forcing"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Surface-Energy-Balance-Diagnostics","page":"Energy Balance","text":"The resolved surface energy balance is accumulated in the state as time integrals in W m⁻² day (positive into the surface), so that dividing by the elapsed days gives a mean flux: shortwave_down_sum, shortwave_net_sum, longwave_down_sum, longwave_net_sum (nonlinear, at the resolved T_s), sensible_heat_flux_sum (at the resolved T_s), rain_heat_flux_sum (only in steps without snowfall) and latent_heat_flux_sum. They are diagnostics only and do not feed back on the solution. The :monthly output group writes them as monthly means in W m⁻² (see Running Simulations).","title":"Surface Energy Balance Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Diffusion","page":"Energy Balance","text":"The vertical diffusion equation is\n\nc_i rho fracpartial Tpartial t\n= fracpartialpartial zleft( K(rho T)fracpartial Tpartial z right)\n\nwhere rho is the layer density and only the solid mass contributes to the heat capacity.","title":"Diffusion"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Thermal-Conductivity","page":"Energy Balance","text":"The conductivity follows Calonne et al. (2019), Eq. (5). It blends regressions for snow and firn and scales them with the temperature dependence of the conductivities of ice and air,\n\nK(rhoT) = left1-f(rho)rightS_mathrmsnow(T)K_mathrmsnow(rho)\n          + f(rho)S_mathrmfirn(T)K_mathrmfirn(rho)\nqquad\nf(rho) = frac11+exp-004(rho-450)\n\nwith rho in kg m⁻³, T in K and K in W m⁻¹ K⁻¹,\n\nbeginaligned\nK_mathrmsnow(rho) = 0024-123times10^-4rho+25times10^-6rho^2\nK_mathrmfirn(rho) = 2107+3618times10^-3(rho-rho_mathrmice)\nS_mathrmsnow(T) = fracK_mathrmice(T)K_mathrmair(T)2107times0024\nqquad S_mathrmfirn(T) = fracK_mathrmice(T)2107\nK_mathrmice(T) = 9828e^-57times10^-3T\nqquad K_mathrmair(T) = 2334times10^-3fracT^3216454+T\nendaligned\n\nThe constants are fixed in the code. There is no user-facing ice conductivity parameter.","title":"Thermal Conductivity"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Interface-Conductance","page":"Energy Balance","text":"For each layer, the code first computes the layer thickness from the layer's solid mass m_i and density rho_i (not the ice density):\n\nDelta z_i = fracm_irho_i\n\nFor the interface between neighboring layers i and i+1, the helper interface_conductance computes\n\nG_i+12\n=\nleft(\nfracDelta z_i2K_i\n+ fracDelta z_i+12K_i+1\nright)^-1\n=\nfrac2K_iK_i+1\nK_i+1Delta z_i + K_iDelta z_i+1\n\nand we have\n\nbeta_i = -fracDelta tc_i m_i\n\nso the off-diagonal coefficients are proportional to beta_i G_ipm12.","title":"Interface Conductance"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Ice-Substrate","page":"Energy Balance","text":"With Layering(ice_substrate_layers > 0) (default 5), the solve continues below the snow/firn layers into a fixed-geometry layer of glacier ice. Substrate layer k has density rho_i and thickness Delta z_k = 2^k-1Delta z_mathrmtop with ice_substrate_top_thickness_m = Delta z_mathrmtop (default 0.05 m, 1.55 m in total for five layers). Its temperatures are stored in model.state.ice_temperature and are solved in the same tridiagonal system as the snow, with the usual interface conductance between the deepest snow layer and the top of the substrate. The base of the substrate is insulated.\n\nWhen a column has no snow, the top substrate layer forms the surface and the same Robin boundary applies, with ice emissivity and roughness. Bare ice therefore cools when the surface energy balance is negative and has to be re-warmed to the melting point before it melts. Melt and rain on bare ice run off. With ice_substrate_layers=0, bare ice is instead held at the melting point and only positive surface energy is used.","title":"Ice Substrate"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Discrete-System","page":"Energy Balance","text":"For interior layers, the implemented stencil can still be understood in the usual tridiagonal form\n\na_i T_i-1^n+1 + b_i T_i^n+1 + c_i T_i+1^n+1 = r_i\n\nwith coefficients assembled from the layer masses, conductivities, and thicknesses. The surface row additionally includes the eliminated Robin boundary term, while the bottom row (the deepest snow layer or the base of the ice substrate) only sees conductive exchange with the layer above.\n\nThe multi-layer system is solved with the Thomas algorithm, eliminating from the bottom row upward. This leaves, for every row below the surface, a relation T_i = p_i + q_i T_i-1 that does not involve the surface boundary condition. Only the surface row differs between a free and a melting surface, so it is closed last as a single scalar equation for T_1, and the profile follows from one downward substitution.","title":"Discrete System"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Melt-Point-Constraint","page":"Energy Balance","text":"If the interface temperature from the free (Robin) surface row exceeds T0, the code:\n\nmarks the step as needing melt\nfixes the interface at T_s = T_0 and closes the surface row again, keeping the conduction from the surface into the first layer, G_s (T_0 - T_1). Rows below the surface are reused from the single upward elimination, so this gives the same profile as a second full solve of the system with the Dirichlet surface row.\nclamps the final profile to T <= T0\nreturns the remaining surface energy as melt energy,\n\nE_mathrmmelt =\nmaxleft(0\nleftQ_mathrmconst - Q_mathrmlin T_0 - G_s (T_0 - T_1)rightDelta t\nright)\n\nThe energy that warms the snow below a melting surface is thus conducted into the column instead of being melted, and no conductive flux leaves an above-melting-point surface.","title":"Melt-Point Constraint"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#Boundary-Conditions","page":"Energy Balance","text":"The surface and bottom closures of the implicit heat solve are the boundary slot of a BESSIModel, BESSIModel(grid; forcing, boundary = BoundaryConditions(surface = …, bottom = …)) ([boundary] in TOML):\n\nBoundary Condition Closure\nsurface SurfaceEnergyBalance() (default) Robin, Q(Ts) = Gs (Ts − T₁) with Gs = 2K₁/Δz₁; Dirichlet T_s = T₀ when the free surface would exceed the melting point, the excess energy melts\nsurface PrescribedSurfaceFlux(F) Neumann, F = Gs (Ts − T₁); melting as above\nsurface PrescribedSurfaceTemperature(T) Dirichlet, Ts = T, flux Gs (T − T₁) into the column, no melt\nbottom Insulated() (default) no flux through the bottom row\nbottom GeothermalFlux(q) Neumann, cᵢ mₙ dTₙ/dt = … + q\nbottom PrescribedBottomTemperature(T) Dirichlet half a layer below the bottom row, flux Gₙ (T − Tₙ) with Gₙ = 2Kₙ/Δzₙ\n\nThe bottom row is that of the ice substrate when the column has one. The energy that crosses either boundary enters boundary_enthalpy, so the enthalpy budget closes for every combination (test/test_boundary_conditions.jl). A value is a number for all columns or a vector with one value per column, which set_boundary!(model, :bottom, values) updates (e.g. the near-surface ice temperature of a coupled ice-sheet model).","title":"Boundary Conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/energy/#API","page":"Energy Balance","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"processes/energy/#Chion.BoundaryConditions","page":"Energy Balance","text":"struct BoundaryConditions{S<:Chion.AbstractSurfaceBoundaryCondition, B<:Chion.AbstractBottomBoundaryCondition}\n\nThe boundary conditions of the column heat solve.\n\nBoundaryConditions(; surface = SurfaceEnergyBalance(), bottom = Insulated())\n\njulia> BoundaryConditions(bottom = GeothermalFlux(0.06)).bottom\nGeothermalFlux(flux = 0.06)\n\nFields\n\nsurface::Chion.AbstractSurfaceBoundaryCondition: surface condition (default SurfaceEnergyBalance())\nbottom::Chion.AbstractBottomBoundaryCondition: bottom condition (default Insulated())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.BoundaryConditions"},{"category":"type","location":"processes/energy/#Chion.SurfaceEnergyBalance","page":"Energy Balance","text":"struct SurfaceEnergyBalance <: Chion.AbstractSurfaceBoundaryCondition\n\nRobin condition from the linearised surface energy balance, Q(Ts) = Gs (Ts − T₁) with Gs = 2K₁/Δz₁, switching to the Dirichlet condition T_s = T₀ when the free surface would exceed the melting point; the excess energy melts snow or ice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.SurfaceEnergyBalance"},{"category":"type","location":"processes/energy/#Chion.PrescribedSurfaceFlux","page":"Energy Balance","text":"struct PrescribedSurfaceFlux{NF} <: Chion.AbstractSurfaceBoundaryCondition\n\nNeumann condition: a constant heat flux F into the surface replaces the energy balance, F = Gs (Ts − T₁); above the melting point the surface is held at T₀ and the excess melts.\n\nFields\n\nflux::Any = 0.0: heat flux into the surface, a number or one value per column (W m⁻², default 0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.PrescribedSurfaceFlux"},{"category":"type","location":"processes/energy/#Chion.PrescribedSurfaceTemperature","page":"Energy Balance","text":"struct PrescribedSurfaceTemperature{NF} <: Chion.AbstractSurfaceBoundaryCondition\n\nDirichlet condition: the surface temperature is fixed, Ts = T, and the heat flux into the column is Gs (T − T₁). No melt is diagnosed; use temperatures at or below T₀.\n\nFields\n\ntemperature::Any = 263.15: surface temperature, a number or one value per column (K, default 263.15)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.PrescribedSurfaceTemperature"},{"category":"type","location":"processes/energy/#Chion.Insulated","page":"Energy Balance","text":"struct Insulated <: Chion.AbstractBottomBoundaryCondition\n\nNo heat flux through the bottom of the column (the default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Insulated"},{"category":"type","location":"processes/energy/#Chion.GeothermalFlux","page":"Energy Balance","text":"struct GeothermalFlux{NF} <: Chion.AbstractBottomBoundaryCondition\n\nNeumann condition: a constant heat flux q enters the bottom layer from below, cᵢ mₙ dTₙ/dt = … + q.\n\nFields\n\nflux::Any = 0.05: upward heat flux into the column, a number or one value per column (W m⁻², default 0.05)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.GeothermalFlux"},{"category":"type","location":"processes/energy/#Chion.PrescribedBottomTemperature","page":"Energy Balance","text":"struct PrescribedBottomTemperature{NF} <: Chion.AbstractBottomBoundaryCondition\n\nDirichlet condition half a layer below the bottom row: the bottom layer exchanges heat Gₙ (T_b − Tₙ) with Gₙ = 2Kₙ/Δzₙ, e.g. with the near-surface ice temperature of an ice-sheet model.\n\nFields\n\ntemperature::Any = 263.15: temperature below the column, a number or one value per column (K, default 263.15)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.PrescribedBottomTemperature"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/energy/#Chion.set_boundary!","page":"Energy Balance","text":"set_boundary!(model, which, values)\n\nSet the per-column values of the :surface or :bottom boundary condition of model (e.g. the near-surface ice temperature of an ice-sheet model each coupling step); the condition must have been built with one value per column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.set_boundary!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/energy/#Chion.go_energy_flux!","page":"Energy Balance","text":"go_energy_flux!(model, idx, air_temperature, shortwave_down, latent_heat_linear_coefficient,\n                latent_heat_constant_term, dt_seconds; scratch = model.cache.workspace.energy,\n                q_sw_net = nothing, q_lw_down = nothing, q_sh = nothing, q_lh = nothing)\n\nThe column energy-flux solve of column idx of a CPU model (for tests and analysis): mutates the model's state and returns the diagnostic named tuple of _go_energy_flux_resolved!. A given flux (q_*) replaces its parameterization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.go_energy_flux!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/energy/#Chion._go_energy_flux_resolved!-processes-energy","page":"Energy Balance","text":"_go_energy_flux_resolved!(..., scratch, air_temperature, shortwave_down, latent_heat_linear_coefficient_eff, latent_heat_constant_term_eff, dt_seconds, use_q_sw_net, q_sw_net_value, use_q_lw_down, q_lw_down_value, use_q_sh, q_sh_value, use_q_lh, q_lh_value, ..., ice_temperature, n_ice, ice_top_thickness)\n\nAdvance the temperature profile of column idx over one time step by solving the surface energy balance and vertical heat diffusion problem. Mutates temperature and surface-temperature state in-place and returns a named tuple of energy diagnostics, including melt availability. With n_ice > 0 the optional ice substrate is solved below the snow/firn layers in the same implicit system; without surface snow its top layer forms the (bare-ice) surface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._go_energy_flux_resolved!"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Parameters","page":"Parameters","text":"All physical constants and scheme choices of a BESSIModel are fields of one immutable struct, Params; the bulk models take PDDParams and ITMParams. The vertical discretisation of the columns is not a parameter but part of the grid, Layering (see Models And Coupling).\n\nEvery exchangeable process is a scheme slot of Params that takes an instance of a scheme type, and the instance carries the coefficients of that scheme. Constants shared by several schemes (the dry, wet and ice albedos, the emissivities, the material constants) stay flat fields of Params:\n\nparams = Params(;\n    NF = Float32,                                           # number type of the model\n    albedo_scheme = AgingAlbedo(cold_timescale_days = 15),  # scheme with a coefficient\n    turbulent_flux_scheme = SemixTurbulentFluxes(sensible_exchange_factor = 2.5),\n    diurnal_cycle = DiurnalSubsteps(max_substeps = 4,\n        temperature_cycle = NoDiurnalTemperatureCycle()),\n    alpha_dry = 0.84,                                       # shared constant\n)\nmodel = BESSIModel(grid; forcing, params)\nmodel.alpha_dry, model.albedo_scheme                        # forwarded to model.params\n\nParams(params; kwargs...) replaces single fields of an existing Params. Unknown keywords throw an error that lists the fields. Every number and the floating-point coefficients of the schemes are converted to NF.\n\nThe defaults are the Greenland calibration against MAR v3.14.3 with daily forcing (1980–1989 for the surface energy balance). They are a regression contract: the paper runs depend on them.","title":"Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Kinks","page":"Parameters","text":"Params(kinks = SmoothKinks(flux, wind, fraction)) smooths the kinks that the state crosses (the bare-ice melt flux max(Q, 0), the wind-speed floor of the SEMIX fluxes, the clamp of the cloud proxy's shortwave deficit) over a band of half-width ε per quantity, for smoother gradients in calibration; the default ExactKinks() keeps them exact (bit-identical).","title":"Kinks"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Named-Setups","page":"Parameters","text":"Setup Domain Differences from Params()\nParamsGrISv1 Greenland none (the defaults)\nParamsAISv1 Antarctica refitted CloudProxyLongwave, SemixTurbulentFluxes with weaker stable damping and a larger latent factor, brighter snow\n\nThe Antarctic setup changes, relative to the Greenland calibration,\n\nField Value\nlongwave_scheme.clear_sky_emissivity 0.7314\nlongwave_scheme.clear_sky_emissivity_temperature_slope 0.003378 K⁻¹\nlongwave_scheme.overcast_emissivity 0.9877\nlongwave_scheme.overcast_emissivity_temperature_slope 0.004171 K⁻¹\nlongwave_scheme.cloud_emissivity_exponent 2.0\nlongwave_scheme.night_cloud_fraction 0.9\nturbulent_flux_scheme.sensible_exchange_factor 2.5\nturbulent_flux_scheme.stable_coefficient 20\nturbulent_flux_scheme.latent_exchange_factor 3.0\nalpha_dry, alpha_wet 0.87, 0.82\n\nKeywords of a named setup override single fields, e.g. ParamsAISv1(NF = Float32, alpha_dry = 0.86). In a configuration file the setup is setup = \"GrISv1\" or setup = \"AISv1\" in [model] (see Running Simulations).","title":"Named Setups"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Schemes","page":"Parameters","text":"Slot of Params Default Alternatives TOML names\nfresh_snow_density_scheme ConstantFreshSnowDensity() ParameterizedFreshSnowDensity() constant, parameterized\nalbedo_scheme DynamicAlbedo() ConstantAlbedo(), AgingAlbedo(), SemixAlbedo(), PrescribedAlbedo() dynamic, constant, aging, semix, prescribed\ndensification_scheme BESSIDensification() HTESSELDensification() bessi, htessel\nsurface_energy_balance_scheme SemixSurfaceEnergyBalance() BESSISurfaceEnergyBalance() semix, bessi\nturbulent_flux_scheme SemixTurbulentFluxes() BESSITurbulentFluxes() semix, bessi\nlongwave_scheme CloudProxyLongwave() GraybodyLongwave() cloud_proxy, graybody\ndiurnal_cycle DiurnalSubsteps() NoDiurnalCycle() substeps, none\n\nThe nested slots are SemixAlbedo(snow_bands = …) (DangSnowAlbedo(), the default, or WarrenWiscombeSnowAlbedo(); TOML dang, warren_wiscombe) and DiurnalSubsteps(temperature_cycle = …) (DiurnalTemperatureCycle(), the default, or NoDiurnalTemperatureCycle(); TOML sinusoidal, none). using InteractiveUtils; subtypes(Chion.AbstractAlbedoScheme) lists the implementations of a slot.","title":"Schemes"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Fresh-snow-density","page":"Parameters","text":"See Accumulation And Melt.","title":"Fresh-snow density"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Albedo","page":"Parameters","text":"See Albedo. The albedos alpha_dry, alpha_wet, alpha_ice and max_lwc_albedo are fields of Params.","title":"Albedo"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Densification","page":"Parameters","text":"See Densification.","title":"Densification"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Surface-energy-balance,-turbulent-fluxes-and-longwave","page":"Parameters","text":"See Energy Balance. The emissivities ϵ_snow, eps_ice and ϵ_air are fields of Params.","title":"Surface energy balance, turbulent fluxes and longwave"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Diurnal-cycle","page":"Parameters","text":"See Diurnal Shortwave Cycle.","title":"Diurnal cycle"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Bulk-Models","page":"Parameters","text":"PDDModel takes PDDParams, whose method slot selects the positive degree days: SimplePDD() (the default) from the positive part of the mean temperature, PISMPDD() from the Calov–Greve expectation integral at every step. ITMModel takes ITMParams, the coefficients of the Fortran insolation–temperature–melt scheme.","title":"Bulk Models"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractKinks","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of how the model treats its kinks, the switches of max, min and clamp. Pass an instance as Params(kinks = …): ExactKinks (the default) or SmoothKinks.\n\nThe kinks make the model piecewise smooth: a derivative exists almost everywhere but jumps where a switch flips, and gradient-based calibration can stall there. Discrete events (layer split and merge, snow ↔ bare ice) are not kinks in this sense and stay as they are.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractKinks"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.ExactKinks","page":"Parameters","text":"struct ExactKinks <: Chion.AbstractKinks\n\nEvery max, min and clamp as it is (the default): bit-identical to a model without a kink treatment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ExactKinks"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.SmoothKinks","page":"Parameters","text":"struct SmoothKinks{NF<:Real} <: Chion.AbstractKinks\n\nSmooth the kinks over a band of half-width ε, one ε per physical quantity: max(x, 0) becomes the C¹ quadratic blend (x + ε)² / 4ε for |x| < ε and is exact outside the band; max(a, b), min(a, b) and clamp follow from it. An ε of zero keeps that group exact.\n\nε where\nflux (W m⁻²) melt energy of bare ice, max(Q_net, 0)\nwind (m s⁻¹) floor of the wind speed (0.1 m s⁻¹) of the SEMIX turbulent fluxes\nfraction (–) clamp of the shortwave deficit of the cloud-proxy longwave to [0, 1]\n\nNot smoothed are bounds that the state sits on rather than crosses, where a blend would bias every step by about ε/4 (albedo at alpha_wet/alpha_dry, temperatures clamped to T₀), and the power of the cloud fraction at zero.\n\nFields\n\nflux::Real = 1.0: half-width for heat fluxes (W m⁻², default 1)\nwind::Real = 0.05: half-width for wind speeds (m s⁻¹, default 0.05)\nfraction::Real = 0.01: half-width for fractions (–, default 0.01)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.SmoothKinks"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.Params","page":"Parameters","text":"struct Params{NF<:Real, FS<:Chion.AbstractFreshSnowDensityScheme, A<:Chion.AbstractAlbedoScheme, D<:Chion.AbstractDensificationScheme, S<:Chion.AbstractSurfaceEnergyBalanceScheme, T<:Chion.AbstractTurbulentFluxScheme, L<:Chion.AbstractLongwaveScheme, DC<:Chion.AbstractDiurnalCycle, K<:Chion.AbstractKinks}\n\nAll physical constants and scheme choices of a BESSIModel, in one immutable, concretely typed struct. Build it with the keyword constructor, which fills in the defaults, the Greenland calibration against MAR v3.14.3 (daily forcing):\n\njulia> params = Params(albedo_scheme = AgingAlbedo(cold_timescale_days = 15), NF = Float32);\n\njulia> params.albedo_scheme\nAgingAlbedo(cold_timescale_days = 15.0)\n\njulia> params.alpha_dry\n0.81f0\n\nEvery scheme slot takes an instance of its abstract type; the instance carries the coefficients of that scheme. ParamsAISv1 gives the Antarctic calibration.\n\nFields\n\nrho_i::Real: ice density (kg m⁻³, default 917)\nrho_w::Real: liquid water density (kg m⁻³, default 1000)\nci::Real: specific heat capacity of ice (J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, default 2110)\ncw::Real: specific heat capacity of liquid water (J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, default 4181)\nLm::Real: latent heat of fusion (J kg⁻¹, default 3.34e5)\nLv::Real: latent heat of vaporisation (J kg⁻¹, default 2.501e6)\ncp_air::Real: specific heat capacity of air (J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, default 1003)\nσ::Real: Stefan–Boltzmann constant (W m⁻² K⁻⁴, default 5.670373e-8)\nT0::Real: melting point (K, default 273.15)\nseconds_per_day::Real: seconds per day (default 86400)\nϵ_snow::Real: longwave emissivity of snow (–, default 0.98)\neps_ice::Real: longwave emissivity of bare ice (–, default 0.98)\nϵ_air::Real: emissivity of a graybody sky, used by GraybodyLongwave (–, default 0.8)\nalpha_dry::Real: albedo of dry fresh snow (–, default 0.81)\nalpha_wet::Real: albedo of wet snow (–, default 0.70)\nalpha_ice::Real: albedo of bare ice (–, default 0.40)\nmax_lwc_albedo::Real: volumetric liquid-water content at which DynamicAlbedo reaches alpha_wet (–, default 0.1)\ndensity_init::Real: density of the initial layers (kg m⁻³, default 300)\ntemperature_init::Real: temperature of the initial layers and substrate (K, default 273)\nrefreezing_correction::Real: factor on the refreezing capacity (–, default 1)\nfresh_snow_density_scheme::Chion.AbstractFreshSnowDensityScheme: density of fresh snow, an AbstractFreshSnowDensityScheme (default ConstantFreshSnowDensity())\nalbedo_scheme::Chion.AbstractAlbedoScheme: snow albedo, an AbstractAlbedoScheme (default DynamicAlbedo())\ndensification_scheme::Chion.AbstractDensificationScheme: densification of low-density snow, an AbstractDensificationScheme (default BESSIDensification())\nsurface_energy_balance_scheme::Chion.AbstractSurfaceEnergyBalanceScheme: longwave exchange of the surface, an AbstractSurfaceEnergyBalanceScheme (default SemixSurfaceEnergyBalance())\nturbulent_flux_scheme::Chion.AbstractTurbulentFluxScheme: turbulent heat fluxes, an AbstractTurbulentFluxScheme (default SemixTurbulentFluxes())\nlongwave_scheme::Chion.AbstractLongwaveScheme: longwave down where the forcing lacks it, an AbstractLongwaveScheme (default CloudProxyLongwave())\ndiurnal_cycle::Chion.AbstractDiurnalCycle: diurnal cycle within a forcing step, an AbstractDiurnalCycle (default DiurnalSubsteps())\nkinks::Chion.AbstractKinks: treatment of the kinks (max, min, clamp), an AbstractKinks (default ExactKinks())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.Params"},{"category":"function","location":"parameters/#Chion.ParamsGrISv1","page":"Parameters","text":"ParamsGrISv1(; NF = Float64, kwargs...)\n\nThe Greenland calibration against MAR v3.14.3 (daily ERA5 forcing, 1980–2019), the defaults of Params. kwargs override single fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ParamsGrISv1"},{"category":"function","location":"parameters/#Chion.ParamsAISv1","page":"Parameters","text":"ParamsAISv1(; NF = Float64, kwargs...)\n\nThe Antarctic calibration against MAR (2010–2019): the Greenland calibration with a refitted cloud-proxy longwave (stage 3 of fit_cloud_proxy_longwave_antarctica.jl), weaker stable damping of the SEMIX turbulent exchange with a larger latent factor, and brighter snow (alpha_dry, alpha_wet). kwargs override single fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ParamsAISv1"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractFreshSnowDensityScheme","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of the density of fresh snow. Pass an instance as Params(fresh_snow_density_scheme = …).\n\nAvailable subtypes: ConstantFreshSnowDensity, ParameterizedFreshSnowDensity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractFreshSnowDensityScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.ConstantFreshSnowDensity","page":"Parameters","text":"struct ConstantFreshSnowDensity{NF<:Real} <: Chion.AbstractFreshSnowDensityScheme\n\nFresh snow takes a constant density, clamped to [50 kg m⁻³, rho_i].\n\nFields\n\ndensity::Real = 315.0: density of fresh snow (kg m⁻³, default 315)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ConstantFreshSnowDensity"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.ParameterizedFreshSnowDensity","page":"Parameters","text":"struct ParameterizedFreshSnowDensity{NF<:Real} <: Chion.AbstractFreshSnowDensityScheme\n\nFresh-snow density from the air temperature Ta and the wind speed U, ρs = a + b (Ta − T₀) + c √U, clamped to [50 kg m⁻³, `rhoi`] (as in Crocus, Vionnet et al., 2012).\n\nFields\n\nintercept::Real = 109.0: density at the melting point without wind, a (kg m⁻³, default 109)\ntemperature_coefficient::Real = 6.0: temperature coefficient b (kg m⁻³ K⁻¹, default 6)\nwind_coefficient::Real = 26.0: wind coefficient c (kg m⁻³ (m s⁻¹)^-½, default 26)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ParameterizedFreshSnowDensity"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractAlbedoScheme","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of the snow albedo. Pass an instance as Params(albedo_scheme = …). The dry, wet and ice albedos alpha_dry, alpha_wet, alpha_ice are fields of Params, since several schemes share them.\n\nAvailable subtypes: DynamicAlbedo, ConstantAlbedo, AgingAlbedo, SemixAlbedo, PrescribedAlbedo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractAlbedoScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.DynamicAlbedo","page":"Parameters","text":"struct DynamicAlbedo <: Chion.AbstractAlbedoScheme\n\nBESSI snow aging: the albedo decreases by (1.35·10⁻³ (T − T₀) + 0.0278) per day, relaxes toward alpha_wet with the liquid-water content of the surface layer (saturated at max_lwc_albedo), and brightens with snowfall, by (alpha_dry − alpha_wet)(1 − exp(−m_s / 3 kg m⁻²)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.DynamicAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.ConstantAlbedo","page":"Parameters","text":"struct ConstantAlbedo <: Chion.AbstractAlbedoScheme\n\nFixed albedos: alpha_dry for a subfreezing snow surface, alpha_wet for a melting one and alpha_ice for bare ice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ConstantAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AgingAlbedo","page":"Parameters","text":"struct AgingAlbedo{NF<:Real} <: Chion.AbstractAlbedoScheme\n\nExponential decay from alpha_dry toward alpha_wet with the time since the latest snowfall, α = αwet + (α − αwet) exp(−Δt / τ), with τ the cold or the melting time scale (Oerlemans and Knap, 1998). Snowfall resets the albedo to alpha_dry.\n\nFields\n\ncold_timescale_days::Real = 20.0: e-folding time of the albedo decay of a subfreezing surface (day, default 20)\nmelting_timescale_days::Real = 2.0: e-folding time of the albedo decay of a melting surface (day, default 2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AgingAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.SemixAlbedo","page":"Parameters","text":"struct SemixAlbedo{NF<:Real, B<:Chion.AbstractSemixSnowAlbedo} <: Chion.AbstractAlbedoScheme\n\nTwo-band snow albedo of the SEMIX surface scheme: visible and near-infrared albedos from the snow grain size, dust content and solar zenith angle, combined with the visible fraction and the cloud cover. Bare ice takes prescribed_ice_albedo where the forcing gives it, else alpha_ice.\n\nFields\n\nsnow_bands::Chion.AbstractSemixSnowAlbedo = DangSnowAlbedo(): spectral snow albedo bands (default DangSnowAlbedo())\nfrac_vu::Real = 0.45: visible fraction of the shortwave down under a clear sky (–, default 0.45)\nalb_snow_vis_new::Real = 0.99: visible albedo of fresh snow (–, default 0.99)\nalb_snow_nir_new::Real = 0.65: near-infrared albedo of fresh snow (–, default 0.65)\nsnow_grain_fresh::Real = 50.0: grain size of fresh snow (µm, default 50)\nsnow_grain_old::Real = 1000.0: grain size of old snow (µm, default 1000)\nd_alb_age_vis::Real = 0.05: visible albedo decrease of old snow (–, default 0.05)\nd_alb_age_nir::Real = 0.25: near-infrared albedo decrease of old snow (–, default 0.25)\nf_age_t::Real = 0.1: temperature factor of the aging rate (K⁻¹, default 0.1)\ndT_age::Real = 0.0: temperature offset of the aging rate (K, default 0)\nsnow_0::Real = 1.0: snowfall that refreshes the surface (kg m⁻² day⁻¹, default 1)\nsnow_1::Real = 0.5: exponent of the snowfall refresh (–, default 0.5)\nw_snow_dust::Real = 10.0: water equivalent over which melt concentrates dust (kg m⁻², default 10)\ndust_con_scale::Real = 1.0: scale of the dust concentration (–, default 1)\ndalb_snow_vis::Real = 0.0: visible albedo offset (–, default 0)\ndalb_snow_nir::Real = 0.0: near-infrared albedo offset (–, default 0)\nk_sigma_orog::Real = 0.0: albedo reduction by sub-grid orography (–, default 0)\nsigma_orog_crit::Real = 1000.0: orography standard deviation of the half reduction (m, default 1000)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.SemixAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractSemixSnowAlbedo","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of the spectral snow albedo of SemixAlbedo.\n\nAvailable subtypes: DangSnowAlbedo, WarrenWiscombeSnowAlbedo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractSemixSnowAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.DangSnowAlbedo","page":"Parameters","text":"Dang et al. (2015) spectral snow albedo bands, including surface roughness.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.DangSnowAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.WarrenWiscombeSnowAlbedo","page":"Parameters","text":"Warren and Wiscombe (1980) spectral snow albedo bands.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.WarrenWiscombeSnowAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.PrescribedAlbedo","page":"Parameters","text":"struct PrescribedAlbedo <: Chion.AbstractAlbedoScheme\n\nThe surface albedo of the prescribed_albedo forcing, which the model requires for every column and step. Where its has_prescribed_albedo mask is false inside the column step (e.g. forcing changed with set_input!), DynamicAlbedo applies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.PrescribedAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractDensificationScheme","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of the densification of low-density snow. Pass an instance as Params(densification_scheme = …).\n\nAvailable subtypes: BESSIDensification, HTESSELDensification.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractDensificationScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.BESSIDensification","page":"Parameters","text":"struct BESSIDensification <: Chion.AbstractDensificationScheme\n\nBESSI densification of snow and firn (Born et al., 2019).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.BESSIDensification"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.HTESSELDensification","page":"Parameters","text":"struct HTESSELDensification <: Chion.AbstractDensificationScheme\n\nHTESSEL low-density densification (Dutra et al., 2010) with compaction by liquid water.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.HTESSELDensification"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractSurfaceEnergyBalanceScheme","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of the longwave exchange of the surface. Pass an instance as Params(surface_energy_balance_scheme = …).\n\nAvailable subtypes: SemixSurfaceEnergyBalance, BESSISurfaceEnergyBalance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractSurfaceEnergyBalanceScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.SemixSurfaceEnergyBalance","page":"Parameters","text":"struct SemixSurfaceEnergyBalance <: Chion.AbstractSurfaceEnergyBalanceScheme\n\nSEMIX graybody exchange: the surface absorbs and emits longwave radiation with the snow or ice emissivity ε, QLW = ε (L↓ − σ Ts⁴).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.SemixSurfaceEnergyBalance"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.BESSISurfaceEnergyBalance","page":"Parameters","text":"struct BESSISurfaceEnergyBalance <: Chion.AbstractSurfaceEnergyBalanceScheme\n\nOriginal BESSI: all incident longwave is absorbed and the snow emissivity enters only the emission, QLW = L↓ − εsnow σ T_s⁴.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.BESSISurfaceEnergyBalance"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractTurbulentFluxScheme","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of the turbulent sensible and latent heat fluxes. Pass an instance as Params(turbulent_flux_scheme = …).\n\nAvailable subtypes: SemixTurbulentFluxes, BESSITurbulentFluxes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractTurbulentFluxScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.SemixTurbulentFluxes","page":"Parameters","text":"struct SemixTurbulentFluxes{NF<:Real} <: Chion.AbstractTurbulentFluxScheme\n\nSEMIX bulk sensible and latent heat fluxes: a neutral transfer coefficient CH = κ² / (ln(z/z₀ₘ) ln(z/z₀ₕ)) corrected with the bulk Richardson number Ri (1/(1 + b Ri) for stable, √(1 − 16 Ri) for unstable stratification), QH = fH ρ cp CH U (Ta − Ts) and QE = fE L ρ CH U (qa − qs).\n\nFields\n\nkarman::Real = 0.4: von Kármán constant κ (–, default 0.4)\nreference_height::Real = 10.0: height z of the air temperature, humidity and wind (m, default 10)\nz0m_snow::Real = 0.001: momentum roughness length of snow (m, default 0.001)\nz0m_ice::Real = 0.01: momentum roughness length of bare ice (m, default 0.01)\nzm_to_zh::Real = 10.0: ratio of the momentum to the heat roughness length z₀ₘ/z₀ₕ (–, default 10)\nsensible_exchange_factor::Real = 3.0: factor f_H of the sensible heat flux (–, default 3, the GrIS calibration)\nstable_coefficient::Real = 40.0: stability coefficient b of stable stratification (–, default 40)\nlatent_exchange_factor::Real = 1.0: factor f_E of the latent heat flux (–, default 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.SemixTurbulentFluxes"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.BESSITurbulentFluxes","page":"Parameters","text":"struct BESSITurbulentFluxes{NF<:Real} <: Chion.AbstractTurbulentFluxScheme\n\nBESSI turbulent fluxes: sensible heat Dsh (Ta − Ts) and latent heat from the vapour-pressure gradient, L (r Dsh / cp) (0.622/p) (ea − es(Ts)).\n\nFields\n\nD_sh::Real = 10.0: sensible heat transfer coefficient D_sh (W m⁻² K⁻¹, default 10)\nlatent_heat_flux_ratio::Real = 1.0: ratio r of the latent to the sensible transfer coefficient (–, default 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.BESSITurbulentFluxes"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractLongwaveScheme","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of the longwave down where the forcing does not give it. Pass an instance as Params(longwave_scheme = …).\n\nAvailable subtypes: CloudProxyLongwave, GraybodyLongwave.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractLongwaveScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.CloudProxyLongwave","page":"Parameters","text":"struct CloudProxyLongwave{NF<:Real} <: Chion.AbstractLongwaveScheme\n\nLongwave down ε σ Ta⁴ with an effective emissivity weighted between clear sky and overcast, ε = εcs (1 − nᵖ) + εoc nᵖ, both linear in Ta − T₀, and the cloud fraction n diagnosed from the shortwave transmissivity: the clear-sky transmissivity τcs = min(1, τ₀ + τz z + τS ln(SWTOA / 300 W m⁻²)) is reduced by clouds by the factor 1 − a nᵇ exp(−d z) (Konzelmann et al., 1994). Resolved once per daily forcing step; in the polar night and for sub-daily forcing n is night_cloud_fraction.\n\nFields\n\nclear_sky_transmissivity_sea_level::Real = 0.718: clear-sky transmissivity τ₀ at sea level and SW_TOA = 300 W m⁻² (–, default 0.718)\nclear_sky_transmissivity_per_km::Real = 0.0298: increase τ_z of the clear-sky transmissivity with height (km⁻¹, default 0.0298)\nclear_sky_transmissivity_insolation::Real = 0.0569: insolation coefficient τ_S of the clear-sky transmissivity (–, default 0.0569)\ncloud_transmission_coefficient::Real = 0.447: cloud transmission coefficient a (–, default 0.447)\ncloud_transmission_exponent::Real = 2.75: cloud transmission exponent b (–, default 2.75)\ncloud_transmission_decay_per_km::Real = 0.225: decay d of the cloud transmission with height (km⁻¹, default 0.225)\nclear_sky_emissivity::Real = 0.716: clear-sky emissivity at T₀ (–, default 0.716)\nclear_sky_emissivity_temperature_slope::Real = 0.00239: temperature slope of the clear-sky emissivity (K⁻¹, default 0.00239)\novercast_emissivity::Real = 0.952: overcast emissivity at T₀ (–, default 0.952)\novercast_emissivity_temperature_slope::Real = 0.00362: temperature slope of the overcast emissivity (K⁻¹, default 0.00362)\ncloud_emissivity_exponent::Real = 3.0: exponent p of the cloud fraction in the emissivity weight (–, default 3)\nnight_cloud_fraction::Real = 0.85: cloud fraction in the polar night and for sub-daily forcing (–, default 0.85)\nemissivity_max::Real = 1.0: upper bound of the effective emissivity (–, default 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.CloudProxyLongwave"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.GraybodyLongwave","page":"Parameters","text":"struct GraybodyLongwave <: Chion.AbstractLongwaveScheme\n\nLongwave down of a graybody sky, ϵair σ Ta⁴ (ϵ_air is a field of Params).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.GraybodyLongwave"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractDiurnalCycle","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of the diurnal cycle within a daily forcing step. Pass an instance as Params(diurnal_cycle = …).\n\nAvailable subtypes: DiurnalSubsteps, NoDiurnalCycle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractDiurnalCycle"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.DiurnalSubsteps","page":"Parameters","text":"struct DiurnalSubsteps{NF<:Real, T<:Chion.AbstractDiurnalTemperatureCycle} <: Chion.AbstractDiurnalCycle\n\nUp to max_substeps column steps per daily forcing step, each with the interval average of the shortwave down reconstructed from the daily mean and the solar geometry (and of the air temperature, with a DiurnalTemperatureCycle). Steps whose shortwave is below threshold or whose air temperature is below min_air_temperature take one step.\n\nFields\n\nmax_substeps::Int64 = 8: maximum number of substeps per forcing step (1 to 24, default 8)\nthreshold::Real = 0.0: daily shortwave below which a step is not split (W m⁻², default 0)\nmin_air_temperature::Real = 273.15 - 8.0: air temperature below which a step is not split (K, default 273.15 − 8)\ntemperature_cycle::Chion.AbstractDiurnalTemperatureCycle = DiurnalTemperatureCycle(): air-temperature cycle of the substeps (default DiurnalTemperatureCycle())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.DiurnalSubsteps"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.NoDiurnalCycle","page":"Parameters","text":"struct NoDiurnalCycle <: Chion.AbstractDiurnalCycle\n\nOne column step per forcing step, with the daily means of the forcing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.NoDiurnalCycle"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractDiurnalTemperatureCycle","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of the air-temperature cycle within the substeps of DiurnalSubsteps.\n\nAvailable subtypes: DiurnalTemperatureCycle, NoDiurnalTemperatureCycle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractDiurnalTemperatureCycle"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.DiurnalTemperatureCycle","page":"Parameters","text":"struct DiurnalTemperatureCycle{NF<:Real} <: Chion.AbstractDiurnalTemperatureCycle\n\nA sinusoidal air-temperature cycle of amplitude A = min(A₀ + γ max(z − zref, 0), Amax) about the daily mean, peaking in the afternoon; each substep takes its interval average.\n\nFields\n\namplitude::Real = 1.0: amplitude A₀ (K, default 1)\namplitude_gradient::Real = 0.0: increase γ of the amplitude with the surface height above reference_height (K m⁻¹, default 0)\nreference_height::Real = 0.0: height z_ref above which the amplitude increases (m, default 0)\namplitude_max::Real = 1.0: upper bound A_max of the amplitude (K, default 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.DiurnalTemperatureCycle"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.NoDiurnalTemperatureCycle","page":"Parameters","text":"struct NoDiurnalTemperatureCycle <: Chion.AbstractDiurnalTemperatureCycle\n\nThe substeps take the daily-mean air temperature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.NoDiurnalTemperatureCycle"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.PDDParams","page":"Parameters","text":"struct PDDParams{NF<:Real, M<:Chion.AbstractPDDMethod}\n\nParameters of a PDDModel: degree-day factors of snow and ice, the refrozen fraction of meltwater, the temperature spread of the positive degree days, and the snow cap above which snow turns into ice.\n\nFields\n\nddf_snow::Real: degree-day factor of snow (mm w.e. K⁻¹ day⁻¹, default 3)\nddf_ice::Real: degree-day factor of ice (mm w.e. K⁻¹ day⁻¹, default 8)\nrefreezing_fraction::Real: fraction of melt and rain that refreezes (–, default 0.6)\ntemperature_sigma::Real: standard deviation of the air temperature (K, default 5)\nH_snow_max::Real: snow water equivalent above which snow becomes ice (mm w.e., default 5000)\nT0::Real: melting point (K, default 273.15)\nseconds_per_day::Real: seconds per day (default 86400)\nmethod::Chion.AbstractPDDMethod: positive degree days, an AbstractPDDMethod (default SimplePDD())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.PDDParams"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.AbstractPDDMethod","page":"Parameters","text":"Abstract supertype of the positive degree days of a PDDModel. Pass an instance as PDDParams(method = …).\n\nAvailable subtypes: SimplePDD, PISMPDD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.AbstractPDDMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.SimplePDD","page":"Parameters","text":"struct SimplePDD <: Chion.AbstractPDDMethod\n\nPositive degree days from the positive part of the mean temperature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.SimplePDD"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.PISMPDD","page":"Parameters","text":"struct PISMPDD <: Chion.AbstractPDDMethod\n\nCalov–Greve expectation integral of a normally distributed temperature (as in PISM).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.PISMPDD"},{"category":"type","location":"parameters/#Chion.ITMParams","page":"Parameters","text":"struct ITMParams{NF<:Real}\n\nParameters of an ITMModel, the insolation–temperature–melt scheme of the Fortran fesmc/chion/smbpal code: melt M = max(τ(z)(1 − α) S + c + t (T − T₀), 0) / (ρw Lm) with the transmissivity τ = a + b √z and a latitude-dependent offset c.\n\nFields\n\ntrans_a::Real: transmissivity at sea level, a (–, default 0.46)\ntrans_b::Real: transmissivity increase with √height, b (m^-½, default 6e-5)\nitm_c::Real: melt offset c (W m⁻², default -45)\nitm_t::Real: temperature coefficient t (W m⁻² K⁻¹, default 10)\nitm_b::Real: latitude slope of the offset (W m⁻² degree⁻¹, default -2)\nitm_lat0::Real: reference latitude of the offset (degree, default 65; 90 or more disables the slope)\nH_snow_max::Real: snow water equivalent above which snow becomes ice (mm w.e., default 5000)\nPmaxfrac::Real: maximum refrozen fraction of the precipitation (–, default 0.6)\nH_snow_crit_desert::Real: snow depth of full snow cover on desert (mm w.e., default 10)\nH_snow_crit_forest::Real: snow depth of full snow cover on forest (mm w.e., default 100)\nmelt_crit::Real: melt rate above which snow counts as wet (mm w.e. day⁻¹, default 0.5)\nalb_ocean::Real: albedo of the ocean (–, default 0.1)\nalb_land::Real: albedo of bare land (–, default 0.2)\nalb_forest::Real: albedo of forest (–, default 0.1)\nalb_ice::Real: albedo of bare ice (–, default 0.4)\nalb_snow_dry::Real: albedo of dry snow (–, default 0.8)\nalb_snow_wet::Real: albedo of wet snow (–, default 0.65)\nfirn_fac::Real: warming of the firn by refreezing (K per mm w.e. day⁻¹, default 0.0266)\nrho_w::Real: liquid water density (kg m⁻³, default 1000)\nLm::Real: latent heat of fusion (J kg⁻¹, default 3.34e5)\nT0::Real: melting point (K, default 273.15)\nseconds_per_day::Real: seconds per day (default 86400)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.ITMParams"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Layer-Structure-And-Basal-Transfer","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"These routines manage the adaptive layer stack and the basal export of excess mass. They are called from accumulation and melt and define much of the model's mass bookkeeping.\n\nThe column is discretised on a mass-following grid with at most Ntot (default 15) layers. Each layer carries solid mass m_i, liquid water m_wi (kg m⁻²), density rho_i and temperature T_i, so the layer thickness is Delta z_i = m_irho_i. Mass leaving the bottom of the column (bottom merges above rho_i and the depth cap) is transferred to the underlying ice and forms the snow-to-ice term of the ice-sheet SMB.\n\nThese are the fields of Layering, part of the grid (Grid(n; layering = Layering(Ntot = 20))):\n\nParameter Default Meaning\nNtot 15 maximum number of snow/firn layers\nmass_max 500 kg m⁻² split threshold\nmass_split 300 kg m⁻² mass moved into the new layer by a split\nmass_min 100 kg m⁻² surface-merge threshold (inactive with near-surface layers)\nnear_surface_layer_max_thicknesses_m (0.02, 0.05, 0.10, 0.30) m target thicknesses of the top four layers\nice_substrate_layers 5 thermal ice layers below the snow\nice_substrate_top_thickness_m 0.05 m thickness of the top substrate layer (doubling downward)","title":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Surface-Split-And-Merge","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"The surface layer is split when mass[1] > mass_max.\n\nlayer 1 keeps surface_mass - mass_split\nthe new layer 2 receives mass_split\nliquid water is partitioned in proportion to the split mass\ndensity and temperature are copied into both layers\n\nThe surface layer is merged or rebalanced when mass[1] < mass_min. This merge is switched off with the default near-surface layers (whenever the first entry of near_surface_layer_max_thicknesses_m is finite), because the remeshing controls the surface layers.\n\nif the top two layers together exceed 2 * mass_split, the surface is topped up to exactly mass_split\notherwise the two top layers are merged completely","title":"Surface Split And Merge"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Bottom-Merge-And-Slot-Creation","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"When all Ntot layers are already active and the surface must split, the code creates a free slot at the bottom:\n\nfor Ntot > 2, it merges the deepest two active layers\nfor very small columns, it depletes the bottom instead\n\nIf a bottom merge would imply density above rho_i, the excess mass is exported to mass_base and smb_ice.","title":"Bottom Merge And Slot Creation"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Continuous-Basal-Depletion","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"continuous_bottom_deplete! removes a requested solid mass from the bottom upward. Partial removal from a layer also removes liquid water in proportion to the removed solid fraction:\n\nDelta m_w = Delta m fracm_wm\n\nRemoved solid mass is accumulated in mass_base and smb_ice; removed liquid water is sent to runoff.","title":"Continuous Basal Depletion"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Snow-Depth-Cap","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"After accumulation, the code applies a snow-depth cap.\n\nThe reference depth is the fixed constant H_mathrmref = 225 m (BESSI_REFERENCE_SNOW_DEPTH_M, the depth of 15 layers of 450 kg m⁻² at 300 kg m⁻³ in the original BESSI). It is independent of Ntot and mass_split, so a reduced layer count can still represent the same maximum physical depth.\n\nIf the active solid snow depth exceeds this reference depth, the code depletes only enough basal mass to remove the excess depth (solid mass goes to the ice, liquid water to runoff):\n\nDelta H_mathrmbase = H_mathrmsolid - H_mathrmref","title":"Snow-Depth Cap"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Near-Surface-Layers","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"The coarse BESSI layers of several hundred kg m⁻² do not resolve the strong near-surface temperature gradients that control the surface temperature and the refreezing of meltwater. near_surface_layer_max_thicknesses_m (default (0.02, 0.05, 0.10, 0.30) m) therefore sets target thicknesses for the four uppermost layers. After accumulation and again at the end of each step (after melt, sublimation, compaction and refreezing), material exceeding these thicknesses is passed to the layer below, and layers thinner than their target are refilled from below (_fill_near_surface_layer_thicknesses!). The remeshing conserves solid mass, liquid water, volume and sensible heat and adds no physical process.\n\nAn Inf entry disables that level. All four Inf recover the original BESSI layering, in which the surface layer can hold up to mass_max and the mass_min surface merge is active.\n\nLayers below the refined near-surface layers (from layer 5 down) are split again when they exceed mass_max (_split_deep_layers!), splitting off mass_split. A full column first merges its two deepest layers and exports any excess above rho_i.","title":"Near-Surface Layers"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Ice-Substrate","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"ice_substrate_layers (default 5) adds a fixed-geometry layer of glacier ice below the snow/firn column. Its top layer is ice_substrate_top_thickness_m (default 0.05 m) thick and thicknesses double downward (1.55 m in total for the defaults). The substrate is thermal only: it is solved in the same implicit heat equation as the snow layers with an insulated base, stores cold content and forms the surface when no snow is left. It is initialised at temperature_init. Bare ice must therefore be re-warmed to the melting point before it melts. Without the substrate, bare ice would be held at the melting point and negative energy fluxes discarded, which overestimates melt on sub-daily time scales. The substrate is impermeable: melt and rain on bare ice run off. Set Layering(ice_substrate_layers=0) for the original bare-ice treatment at the melting point.","title":"Ice Substrate"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/layer_structure/#API","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Chion.continuous_bottom_deplete!","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"continuous_bottom_deplete!(model, idx, d_m_in)\n\nBasal depletion of column idx of a CPU model. Mutates its state in place and returns a named tuple with the transferred ice and runoff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion.continuous_bottom_deplete!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Chion._split_surface_layer!-processes-layer_structure","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"_split_surface_layer!(N_storage, mass, mass_w, density, temperature, idx, Ntot, mass_max, mass_split)\n\nSplit the surface layer of column idx into two layers when it exceeds the configured mass threshold. Mutates layer arrays and active-layer count in-place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._split_surface_layer!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Chion._merge_surface_layer!-processes-layer_structure","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"_merge_surface_layer!(N_storage, mass, mass_w, density, temperature, idx, Ntot, mass_split, mass_min, c)\n\nMerge or rebalance the top two layers of column idx when the surface layer falls below the minimum target mass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._merge_surface_layer!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Chion._merge_bottom_layer!-processes-layer_structure","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"_merge_bottom_layer!(N_storage, mass, mass_w, density, temperature, mass_base, smb_ice, idx, c)\n\nMerge the two deepest active layers of column idx, exporting any density excess beyond pure ice into basal mass and SMB diagnostics. Returns the enthalpy of the exported ice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._merge_bottom_layer!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Chion._continuous_bottom_deplete!-processes-layer_structure","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"_continuous_bottom_deplete!(N_storage, mass, mass_w, density, temperature, mass_base, smb_ice, runoff, Tsrf, albedo_dynamic, idx, d_m_in, c)\n\nContinuously remove d_m_in of solid mass from the bottom of column idx, routing associated liquid water to runoff and updating basal mass diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._continuous_bottom_deplete!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Chion._enforce_snow_depth_cap!-processes-layer_structure","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"_enforce_snow_depth_cap!(N_storage, mass, mass_w, density, temperature, mass_base, smb_ice, runoff, Tsrf, albedo_dynamic, idx, Ntot, mass_split, dt_seconds, c)\n\nApply the column depth-cap rule after accumulation, removing excess basal mass when the active solid snow depth exceeds the configured cap. Returns the enthalpy of the ice exported to the ice reservoir.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._enforce_snow_depth_cap!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Chion._fill_near_surface_layer_thicknesses!-processes-layer_structure","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"_fill_near_surface_layer_thicknesses!(..., target_thicknesses_m, c)\n\nRefill under-thickness near-surface layers from the material immediately below.  This is the upward half of conservative remeshing: solid mass, liquid water, volume, and sensible enthalpy are preserved.  A shallow column is not padded; its deepest active layer may remain thinner than its target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._fill_near_surface_layer_thicknesses!"},{"category":"function","location":"processes/layer_structure/#Chion._split_deep_layers!-processes-layer_structure","page":"Layer Structure And Basal Transfer","text":"_split_deep_layers!(N_storage, mass, mass_w, density, temperature, mass_base, smb_ice, idx, Ntot, first_deep, mass_max, mass_split, c)\n\nKeep the firn below the geometrically capped near-surface layers resolved. The near-surface remeshing passes all excess material into layer first_deep; any layer from first_deep on that exceeds mass_max is split into an upper part and a lower part of mass_split (the standard BESSI layer mass). A full column first merges its two deepest layers, as accumulation does. The two deepest layers of a full column are never split, so the deep bulk layer may grow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chion._split_deep_layers!"},{"category":"section","location":"#Chion.jl","page":"Home","text":"Chion is a fast, intermediate-complexity snowpack model for ice sheets and seasonal snow, written in Julia for CPUs and GPUs. It combines a multilayer snow and firn model with two bulk surface mass balance models:\n\nBESSIModel, the multilayer model. It solves the coupled surface energy balance, heat conduction, densification, percolation and refreezing in independent vertical columns. Its structure follows the BErgen Snow SImulator (BESSI; Born et al., 2019; Zolles and Born, 2021). It extends BESSI with a bulk-aerodynamic turbulent-flux scheme, a parameterised longwave radiation, a thermal ice substrate, a refined near-surface grid and energy-conserving diurnal substeps.\nPDDModel, a positive-degree-day model with a single snow reservoir.\nITMModel, the insolation–temperature–melt model (van den Berg et al., 2008; Robinson et al., 2010).\n\n(Image: Schematic of the multilayer snowpack model)\n\n(a) Forcing and the layered column: four refined near-surface layers, up to 15 snow and firn layers and five thermal ice-substrate layers. (b) The order of processes within one (sub)step. (c) Typical use: forcing from MAR, ERA5 or CMIP6 drives Chion on the GPU or CPU, optionally coupled to an ice-sheet model.\n\nThe minimal forcing is near-surface air temperature, downwelling shortwave radiation, and snowfall and rainfall. Relative humidity, wind speed, air pressure, surface elevation and latitude are optional. Each component of the surface energy balance, the albedo and the melt can also be prescribed, for example from a regional climate model. The default parameters were calibrated for the Greenland ice sheet against MAR with daily forcing.\n\nnote: Note\nThe model is still work in progress and the API may change.","title":"Chion.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"path/to/Chion.jl\")\nPkg.instantiate()\nusing Chion\n\nNetCDF input and output use NCDatasets.jl (installed with Chion). The GPU backend needs CUDA.jl, a weak dependency: add it to your environment (Pkg.add(\"CUDA\")) and load it with using CUDA before building a model with backend = CUDABackend(). Configuration files run with run_config load it automatically.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"A single column with two days of hand-written forcing:\n\nusing Chion\n\nforcing = SnowpackForcing(\n    dt_days=[1.0, 1.0],\n    air_temperature_c=[-12.0, -10.0],\n    snowfall_mm_day=[1.0, 0.0],\n    rainfall_mm_day=[0.0, 0.0],\n    shortwave_down=[120.0, 160.0],\n    latitude_deg=72.0,          # used by the diurnal substeps and the longwave\n)\nmodel = BESSIModel(Grid(1); forcing)\nsim = Simulation(model)\nrun!(sim)\nmodel.temperature            # the final state: layer temperatures (K), …\n\nA gridded run from yearly MAR files with a spin-up and several outputs:\n\nusing Chion\nusing CUDA                                  # only for the GPU\n\nforcing = Forcing(\"/data/MAR/MARv3.14.3-10km-daily-ERA5-{year}.nc\";\n    surface_fluxes=:parameterized)          # Chion computes the energy balance\nmodel = BESSIModel(forcing;                 # the grid of the forcing columns\n    params=Params(),                        # the Greenland calibration; ParamsAISv1() for Antarctica\n    backend=CUDABackend())                  # CPU() by default\n\nsim = Simulation(model;\n    years=1940:2026,\n    spinup=CycledSpinup(1940:1969; cycles=5),\n    output=OutputConfig(dir=\"output/greenland\",     # run directory: outputs, log, restarts\n        outputs=[Output(\"smb_monthly.nc\"; variables=:monthly, frequency=:monthly),\n                 Output(\"budget_yearly.csv\"; variables=:budget, frequency=:yearly,\n                        aggregate=:area_sum)]))\nrun!(sim)\n\nRunning Simulations describes this workflow and the equivalent TOML configuration files.","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-To-Go-Next","page":"Home","text":"If you want to Read\nrun the model on files, with spin-up and outputs Running Simulations\ndrive it step by step (coupling, GPUs, custom loops) Models And Coupling\nunderstand the state and the order of processes Model State And Step Flow\nlook up the physics of one process the Processes pages, e.g. Energy Balance\nfind a default value or choose a scheme Parameters\nsee how the numerics are verified Verification","title":"Where To Go Next"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/fresh_snow_density/#Fresh-Snow-Density-Validation-Note","page":"Fresh-Snow Density Test","text":"This page records the fresh-snow-density formula used by the current codebase and the analytical spot checks that were used when documenting it. These notes are retained as validation material; they are not currently backed by a file in test/.","title":"Fresh-Snow Density Validation Note"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/fresh_snow_density/#Parameterization","page":"Fresh-Snow Density Test","text":"This formula applies only with Params(fresh_snow_density_scheme = ParameterizedFreshSnowDensity()). The default ConstantFreshSnowDensity() uses density = 315 kg m^-3 (see Accumulation And Melt). With the parameterized scheme, fresh-snow density is\n\nrho_mathrmfresh =\noperatornameclampleft(\na + b(T_mathrmair - T_0) + csqrtV\n50\nrho_i\nright)\n\nwith implementation defaults\n\na = 109mathrmkgm^-3\nb = 6mathrmkgm^-3K^-1\nc = 26\n\nwhere V is the wind speed and the final result is clamped to 50 rho_i.","title":"Parameterization"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/fresh_snow_density/#Analytical-Checks","page":"Fresh-Snow Density Test","text":"","title":"Analytical Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/fresh_snow_density/#Default-wind-speed-behavior","page":"Fresh-Snow Density Test","text":"SnowpackForcing uses wind_speed = 5.0 m s^-1 when no explicit wind speed is supplied. The generic forcing-file loader uses the same default unless wind_default is changed.","title":"Default wind-speed behavior"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/fresh_snow_density/#Formula-check","page":"Fresh-Snow Density Test","text":"For\n\nT_mathrmair = T_0 + 2mathrmK\nqquad\nV = 9mathrmms^-1\n\nthe expected fresh-snow density is\n\nrho_mathrmfresh = 109 + 6 cdot 2 + 26 cdot sqrt9 = 199mathrmkgm^-3\n\nThis is the value the current _fresh_snow_density implementation produces.","title":"Formula check"},{"category":"section","location":"tests/fresh_snow_density/#Diagnostic-Figure","page":"Fresh-Snow Density Test","text":"(Image: Fresh-snow density versus wind speed)","title":"Diagnostic Figure"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/diurnal_cycle/#Diurnal-Shortwave-Cycle","page":"Diurnal Shortwave Cycle","text":"Chion can subdivide a daily BESSI forcing step to resolve the daytime shortwave peak. The scheme reconstructs an idealized solar cycle whose full-day mean equals the supplied shortwave forcing. An optional sinusoidal air-temperature cycle can be applied over the same substeps.\n\nDaily-mean forcing underestimates melt: around local noon the shortwave flux can exceed the energy needed for melt even when the daily mean does not. The substeps capture this without sub-daily forcing. They redistribute shortwave energy within the day but never change its daily total.\n\nThe feature is enabled by default (calibrated for daily GrIS forcing) and applies only to BESSIModel. It is the diurnal_cycle slot of Params: DiurnalSubsteps() (the default) or NoDiurnalCycle().\n\n(Image: Reconstructed diurnal cycle of the shortwave radiation)\n\nReconstructed shortwave flux (blue) for a daily mean of 200 W m⁻² (dashed) at 40, 50, 60 and 70 °N at the June solstice, and its interval means for 4, 8, 12 and 24 substeps. The mean over the day equals the daily mean for any number of substeps. The default uses 8.","title":"Diurnal Shortwave Cycle"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/diurnal_cycle/#Configuration","page":"Diurnal Shortwave Cycle","text":"The defaults correspond to:\n\nparams = Params(diurnal_cycle=DiurnalSubsteps(\n    max_substeps=8,\n    threshold=0.0,\n    min_air_temperature=273.15 - 8.0,\n    temperature_cycle=DiurnalTemperatureCycle(amplitude=1.0),\n))\n\nSet diurnal_cycle=NoDiurnalCycle() for forcing that is already sub-daily.\n\nThe coefficients mean:\n\nCoefficient Default Meaning\nDiurnalSubsteps(threshold) 0 W m^-2 required noon-peak-minus-mean excess of the selected shortwave (q_sw_net if prescribed, otherwise shortwave_down)\nDiurnalSubsteps(max_substeps) 8 exact number of substeps when subdivision activates; valid range 1–24\nDiurnalSubsteps(min_air_temperature) 265.15 K (−8 °C) subdivision requires daily mean air temperature above this value\nDiurnalSubsteps(temperature_cycle) DiurnalTemperatureCycle() reconstruct a diurnal air-temperature cycle; NoDiurnalTemperatureCycle() disables it\nDiurnalTemperatureCycle(amplitude) 1 K half-amplitude A_0 of that temperature cycle\nDiurnalTemperatureCycle(amplitude_gradient) 0 K m^-1 increase g of the amplitude with surface height\nDiurnalTemperatureCycle(reference_height) 0 m height z_mathrmref above which the gradient acts\nDiurnalTemperatureCycle(amplitude_max) 1 K upper bound A_max of the amplitude\n\nFinite latitude_deg forcing is required when diurnal shortwave substeps are enabled. Solar longitude is derived from each forcing timestamp.","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/diurnal_cycle/#Solar-Geometry","page":"Diurnal Shortwave Cycle","text":"For latitude phi and solar longitude lambda, the implementation approximates solar declination as\n\ndelta =\nsin^-1left(sinepsilonsinlambdaright)\nqquad\nepsilon = 23439291^circ\n\nThe sunset hour angle is\n\nh_0 =\ncos^-1left(-tanphitandeltaright)\n\nwith explicit polar-night and polar-day limits of 0 and pi.\n\nFor hour angle h, the unscaled solar shape is\n\nmu(h) =\nsinphisindelta + cosphicosdeltacos h\n\nOnly the daylight interval -h_0h_0 contributes. Its integral is\n\nI_mathrmday =\n2left\nh_0sinphisindelta +\ncosphicosdeltasin h_0\nright","title":"Solar Geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/diurnal_cycle/#Energy-Conserving-Shortwave-Reconstruction","page":"Diurnal Shortwave Cycle","text":"Let overlineQ_sw be the supplied daily mean shortwave flux. Chion scales the solar shape by\n\nS = overlineQ_swfrac2piI_mathrmday\n\nFor a substep spanning hour angles h_ah_b, define the daylight-clipped bounds\n\nd_a = max(h_a-h_0)\nqquad\nd_b = min(h_bh_0)\n\nWhen d_bd_a, the interval-mean shortwave is\n\noverlineQ_swab =\nfracSh_b-h_a\nleft\n(d_b-d_a)sinphisindelta +\ncosphicosdelta(sin d_b-sin d_a)\nright\n\nNighttime intervals receive zero shortwave. Integrating all intervals over -pipi recovers the original daily mean, so subdivision does not alter the prescribed daily shortwave energy.\n\nIf net shortwave q_sw_net is prescribed, the same reconstruction is applied to it. Otherwise it is applied to shortwave_down, and albedo is handled by the normal surface energy calculation.","title":"Energy-Conserving Shortwave Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/diurnal_cycle/#Activation-Logic","page":"Diurnal Shortwave Cycle","text":"For each column and forcing step, Chion first estimates the noon peak\n\nQ_mathrmpeak =\nSleft(sinphisindelta+cosphicosdeltaright)\n\nThe step is subdivided only when all of the following are true:\n\ndiurnal substeps are enabled\nmax_substeps > 1\nthe forcing duration is between 0.75 and 1.25 days\nthe selected daily mean shortwave is positive\ndaily mean air temperature is strictly above the configured minimum\nair temperature, latitude, and solar longitude are finite\nQ_peak - Q_mean is strictly greater than threshold\nthe column is not in polar night\n\nIf every condition passes, the code uses exactly max_substeps; otherwise it uses one normal forcing step. It does not select an intermediate number of substeps.","title":"Activation Logic"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/diurnal_cycle/#Optional-Temperature-Cycle","page":"Diurnal Shortwave Cycle","text":"With temperature_cycle=DiurnalTemperatureCycle(), the reconstructed temperature follows a cosine cycle centered on the supplied daily mean:\n\nT(h) = overlineT + A_Tcos h\nqquad\nA_T = minleftA_max A_0 + gmax(z_s - z_mathrmref 0)right\n\nwith the hour angle h in -pi pi (h = 0 at local noon). By default A_T = 1 K everywhere. Forcing without a surface height gets no elevation term. The elevation dependence follows the diurnal temperature range over Greenland in SEMIC (Krapp et al., 2017): DiurnalTemperatureCycle(amplitude=2.0, amplitude_gradient=0.001, amplitude_max=5.0) reproduce A_T = min(5mathrmK 2mathrmK + 1mathrmKkm^-1z_s).\n\nThe value passed to a substep is its interval average,\n\noverlineT_ab =\noverlineT +\nA_Tfracsin h_b-sin h_ah_b-h_a\n\nThis places the warmest point at solar noon and preserves the full-day mean temperature. The temperature cycle only acts on days that are subdivided by the shortwave criteria above. A day computed as one interval uses the daily mean. With NoDiurnalTemperatureCycle(), every substep uses the original air temperature.","title":"Optional Temperature Cycle"},{"category":"section","location":"processes/diurnal_cycle/#Substep-Forcing","page":"Diurnal Shortwave Cycle","text":"The full day is divided uniformly in hour angle from -pi to pi. Each substep receives:\n\na timestep scaled by its fraction of the day\nreconstructed interval-mean shortwave\noptionally reconstructed interval-mean air temperature\nunchanged precipitation rates and other atmospheric forcing\n\nBecause precipitation remains a rate while the timestep is shortened, total daily snowfall and rainfall are preserved. Incident longwave from the default cloud-proxy scheme is derived once from the daily forcing before the split, so every substep uses the same daily cloudiness. Prescribed melt (prescribed_melt) is distributed over the substeps in proportion to the reconstructed net shortwave when q_sw_net is prescribed and positive, and otherwise in proportion to the substep length. Both preserve the daily total. Each substep then executes the normal BESSI column process sequence.","title":"Substep Forcing"}]
}
